20	Game Theory — Calculus Edition	9	Minimax Theorem	exact_linear	variational	Equality of two optimisation orders for a bilinear form.	Exactly LP strong duality — the max problem and the min problem are dual linear programmes, and the theorem is that their optima coincide. Von Neumann’s theorem and LP duality were later recognised as the same result.	The theorem is LP duality.	Exact linear.
20	Game Theory — Calculus Edition	10	Zero-Sum Game LP	exact_linear	variational	A linear programme whose optimum is the game value.	Solvable in polynomial time, which puts zero-sum equilibrium in P — in sharp contrast to general-sum games. The dual LP gives the opponent’s optimal strategy, so both players’ strategies come from one primal–dual solve.	The game is a linear programme.	Exact linear.
20	Game Theory — Calculus Edition	11	Correlated Equilibrium	exact_linear	probabilistic	A joint distribution satisfying linear inequalities.	The constraints are linear in the joint distribution, so the set of correlated equilibria is a polytope — computable by linear programming in polynomial time. Nash equilibria are the rank-one (product) points of that polytope, and it is exactly the rank-one restriction that makes Nash hard.	Correlated equilibrium is a polytope; Nash is its rank-one subset.	Exact linear. The cleanest illustration in the field of why Nash is hard.
20	Game Theory — Calculus Edition	12	Price of Anarchy	exact_linear	structural	A ratio of two optimisation values.	For aﬀine congestion games PoA = 4/3, proved by a smoothness argument that is essentially a quadratic inequality on the cost vectors. The bound is a statement about a quadratic form dominating a bilinear one.	Smoothness arguments are quadratic inequalities.	Exact linear for aﬀine costs.
20	Game Theory — Calculus Edition	13	Braess’s Paradox	exact_linear	variational,dynamical	A flow problem where the equilibrium and optimum differ.	Equilibrium flow minimises ∑𝑒∫ 𝑓𝑒 0 𝑐𝑒while the social optimum minimises ∑𝑒𝑓𝑒𝑐𝑒(𝑓𝑒) — two different convex objectives over the same flow polytope. The paradox is that enlarging the feasible set can worsen the first while the second improves.	Both are convex programmes on a flow polytope.	Exact linear in the constraint set.
20	Game Theory — Calculus Edition	14	Potential Game	exact_linear	variational,control	A single scalar function whose differences reproduce all payoff differences.	Existence of Φ is an exactness condition: the payoff-difference field must be curl-free. In the Hodge decomposition of games, potential games are the gradient component and zero-sum games the harmonic one — every game decomposes uniquely into the two plus a nonstrategic part.	Potential existence is a curl-free condition.	Exact linear.
