57	Computational Neuroscience — Calculus Edition	43	Linear–Nonlinear–Poisson Model	exact_linear	spectral,operator,probabilistic,dynamical	A linear filter followed by a static nonlinearity.	The filter k is a single direction in stimulus space — a rank-one projection — and everything the model sees is that one-dimensional projection. Spike-triggered covariance extends it to multiple filters by finding the eigenvectors of the spike-triggered covariance that differ from the prior.	The model is a projection; multi-filter extensions are eigenvector problems.	Exact linear (the filter).
57	Computational Neuroscience — Calculus Edition	44	Generalised Linear Model for Spikes	exact_linear	dynamical	A Poisson GLM with a log link.	Exactly a linear predictor exponentiated, and the log-likelihood is concave — so fitting has a unique global optimum. The history term captures refractoriness and bursting within the same linear framework, which is why the GLM displaced ad hoc spike models.	The model is linear in log rate with a concave likelihood.	Exact linear in logs.
57	Computational Neuroscience — Calculus Edition	45	Mutual Information of a Neural Code	exact_linear	operator,probabilistic,informational,dynamical,learning	A divergence between a joint distribution and its rank-one approximation.	Mutual information measures exactly how far the joint distribution is from the outer product of its marginals — the same rank-one comparison as in every independence test here. It is zero precisely when the joint matrix has rank one.	Mutual information measures departure from rank one.	Exact linear.
57	Computational Neuroscience — Calculus Edition	46	Direct Method Information Rate	exact_linear	probabilistic,informational,control	A difference of two entropies.	Exactly a linear contrast between the response entropy and the conditional entropy, both estimated from word distributions. Finite-sample bias in entropy estimation is severe and is corrected by extrapolation in 1/𝑁— a linear extrapolation.	The rate is an entropy difference with a linear bias correction.	Exact linear.
57	Computational Neuroscience — Calculus Edition	47	Fisher Information for Neural Populations	exact_linear	probabilistic,learning	A sum of squared tuning-curve slopes — a Gram-like quantity.	Information comes from tuning-curve slopes, not peaks, so the most informative neurons about a stimulus are those tuned to its flank. With correlated noise, 𝐽= f′⊤Σ−1f′ — a quadratic form with the inverse noise covariance, and correlations can either help or hurt depending on their alignment with f′.	Fisher information is a quadratic form with an inverse covariance.	Exact linear.
57	Computational Neuroscience — Calculus Edition	48	Population Vector Decoding	exact_linear	variational,informational,parallel	A normalised weighted sum of preferred direction vectors. The LKVexa Compendium — Calculus Edition 48	Exactly a weighted average — an inner product with the firing rate vector as weights. It is optimal only when tuning is cosine and preferred directions are uniformly distributed; otherwise it is a biased linear estimator.	Population vector decoding is a weighted vector sum.	Exact linear.
57	Computational Neuroscience — Calculus Edition	49	Maximum Likelihood Decoding	exact_linear	informational	A maximisation of a log-likelihood linear in the spike counts.	Linear in r for fixed 𝜃, so the log-likelihood surface is an inner product of the rate vector with a 𝜃-dependent template — matched filtering across templates. It attains the Cramér–Rao bound asymptotically, which is entry 47.	Decoding is matched filtering across a template family. The LKVexa Compendium — Calculus Edition 49	Exact linear.
57	Computational Neuroscience — Calculus Edition	50	Signal Detection d-Prime	exact_linear	operator,variational,geometric	A standardised mean difference.	Exactly the projection of the mean difference onto the optimal discriminant direction, normalised by noise — Fisher’s linear discriminant in one dimension. In the multivariate case 𝑑′2 = Δ𝜇⊤Σ−1Δ𝜇, a Mahalanobis distance.	𝑑′ is a Mahalanobis distance along a discriminant direction.	Exact linear.
57	Computational Neuroscience — Calculus Edition	51	Neurometric–Psychometric Comparison	exact_linear	geometric	A comparison of two discriminability measures.	The surprising near-equality implies that pooling many neurons yields little improvement, which happens when noise is correlated along the discriminant direction — information-limiting correlations. It is a statement about the alignment of Σ with Δ𝜇.	The result reflects noise correlations aligned with the signal direction.	Exact linear.
57	Computational Neuroscience — Calculus Edition	52	Eﬀicient Coding Principle	exact_linear	operator,variational,probabilistic,informational,dynamical	An information maximisation over a linear encoding.	For Gaussian signals the optimal linear code whitens the input — decorrelating and equalising variance, which is exactly PCA followed by scaling. Retinal centre-surround receptive fields are the whitening filter for natural image statistics.	Eﬀicient coding is whitening — a linear transform of the covariance.	Exact linear.
57	Computational Neuroscience — Calculus Edition	53	Predictive Coding Error	exact_linear	operator,variational,informational	A residual after subtracting a prediction.	Exactly a projection residual: the prediction is a linear map of context, and only the orthogonal component is transmitted. This decorrelates the signal, which is the same whitening objective as entry 52 arrived at from a different direction.	Predictive coding transmits a projection residual.	Exact linear.
57	Computational Neuroscience — Calculus Edition	54	Free Energy Principle	exact_linear	operator,variational,probabilistic,informational	A variational bound — a KL divergence plus a log evidence.	Under Gaussian assumptions the whole scheme reduces to precision-weighted prediction error minimi- sation, which is generalised least squares with the precision matrix as the weight. That Gaussian case is where the principle makes contact with everything else in this chapter.	The Gaussian case is precision-weighted least squares.	Exact linear (Gaussian case).
57	Computational Neuroscience — Calculus Edition	55	Reinforcement Learning Prediction Error	exact_linear	probabilistic,learning	A temporal difference — a linear contrast of value estimates.	With linear function approximation 𝑉= w⊤𝜙, the TD update is a stochastic linear iteration whose fixed point solves a linear system. Dopamine’s correspondence to 𝛿is the field’s most cited link between an algorithm and a signal.	TD learning with linear features is a stochastic linear iteration.	Exact linear.
57	Computational Neuroscience — Calculus Edition	56	BOLD Haemodynamic Response Function	exact_linear	operator,dynamical,learning	A convolution kernel — the impulse response of the vascular system.	The BOLD signal is the neural activity convolved with ℎ, so the whole forward model is a linear time-invariant filter. Its low-pass character is why fMRI has poor temporal resolution regardless of sampling rate.	BOLD is a linear convolution.	Exact linear.
57	Computational Neuroscience — Calculus Edition	57	General Linear Model for fMRI	exact_linear	operator,dynamical,exact_numeric	A design matrix built by convolving the task regressors with the HRF.	Literally ordinary (or generalised) least squares, fitted independently at every voxel — hundreds of thousands of identical linear solves. Contrasts c⊤̂𝛽test hypotheses, and design eﬀiciency is the conditioning of 𝑋⊤𝑋.	fMRI analysis is mass-univariate least squares.	Exact linear. The field’s workhorse.
57	Computational Neuroscience — Calculus Edition	58	Random Field Theory Correction	exact_linear	combinatorial,geometric	An expected Euler characteristic of a thresholded field.	Accounts for spatial smoothness so the correction is far less severe than Bonferroni — the effective number of independent tests is the resel count, which is a smoothness-derived rank. It is a geometric rather than combinatorial correction.	The correction replaces test count with an effective smoothness-derived rank.	Exact linear.
57	Computational Neuroscience — Calculus Edition	59	Dynamic Causal Modelling	exact_linear	probabilistic,dynamical	A bilinear state-space model with three parameter matrices.	𝐴is the intrinsic connectivity, 𝐵𝑗the modulation by input 𝑗, 𝐶the direct input effect — bilin- ear because inputs multiply states. Model comparison is Bayesian, and the estimated 𝐴is directed effective connectivity.	DCM is a bilinear state-space model.	Exact linear (bilinear).
57	Computational Neuroscience — Calculus Edition	60	Granger Causality	exact_linear	variational,dynamical,learning	A log ratio of residual variances from two nested regressions.	Both models are vector autoregressions — linear predictors of the present from the past — and the statistic compares their residual variances. It measures predictive improvement, not causation, and its interpretation degrades badly with unobserved common inputs or differential filtering.	Granger causality is a nested VAR variance comparison.	Exact linear.
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	1	Asymptotic Notation	structural_analogue	structural	An equivalence-class construction on growth rates.	The set of functions of a given order forms a cone closed under addition and positive scaling — so 𝑂(⋅) classes are closed under the operations a vector space would need, but the ordering is a preorder, not a norm. It is a quotient by scaling, not a linear structure.	Growth classes form a cone under a preorder.	Structural analogue.
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	2	Master Theorem	exact_linear	geometric	A divide-and-conquer recurrence with three regimes.	The comparison is between 𝑛log𝑏𝑎and 𝑓(𝑛) — a comparison of two exponents, hence linear in the logs. The three cases correspond to which term dominates the geometric series of the recursion tree.	The cases compare exponents, which is linear in logs.	Exact linear in logs.
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	3	Akra–Bazzi Theorem	exact_linear	structural	A generalisation handling unequal subproblem sizes.	The exponent 𝑝solves ∑𝑎𝑖𝑏𝑝 𝑖= 1 — a single scalar equation, and the integral accumulates the non-recursive work weighted by depth. It is a weighted linear accumulation over the recursion tree.	The solution is a weighted integral with an exponent from a scalar equation.	Exact linear.
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	4	Merge Sort Recurrence	exact_linear	variational	A balanced recursion tree with equal work per level.	Each of the log 𝑛levels does 𝑂(𝑛) work, so the total is a sum of equal terms — a uniform linear accumulation over depth. The optimality follows from entry 5.	The cost is a uniform sum over log 𝑛levels.	Exact linear.
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	5	Comparison Sort Lower Bound	structural_analogue	combinatorial	A counting bound — a decision tree must have 𝑛! leaves.	A binary tree with 𝑛! leaves has depth at least log2 𝑛! ≈𝑛log 𝑛by Stirling. It is an information- theoretic counting argument, and its content is combinatorial rather than linear.	The bound is a leaf-counting argument on a binary tree.	Structural analogue.
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	6	Quicksort Expected Time	exact_linear	structural	An expectation over random pivot choices.	Computed by linearity of expectation over indicator variables for each pair comparison — the sum ∑𝑖<𝑗2/(𝑗−𝑖+ 1) is a harmonic sum. Linearity of expectation is the one genuinely linear tool doing the work here.	The analysis uses linearity of expectation over pair indicators.	Exact linear.
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	7	Binary Search	exact_linear	geometric,exact_numeric	Repeated halving of an interval.	Each step is a contraction by a factor of two, so the number of steps is a logarithm — the reciprocal of a contraction rate. It is the same structure as any geometric convergence.	Search is geometric contraction; cost is a log.	Exact linear in logs.
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	8	Amortised Analysis (Aggregate Method)	exact_linear	combinatorial	An average over a sequence — a linear functional of the cost vector.	Exactly a mean, so expensive operations are paid for by cheap ones. Its validity depends only on summing, which makes it a linear accounting identity.	Amortisation is an average of a cost vector.	Exact linear.
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	9	Potential Method	exact_linear	graph	A telescoping sum with a potential function.	The potential differences telescope exactly, so the total amortised cost differs from the actual by only the endpoint potentials. It is the discrete analogue of a conservative field — path independence from an exact difference.	The method is a telescoping (exact-difference) argument.	Exact linear.
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	10	Union-Find Complexity	structural_analogue	graph,combinatorial	An inverse Ackermann bound — effectively constant.	Path compression flattens the forest, and the analysis assigns ranks and charges operations against them. It is a combinatorial amortised argument with no linear content.	The bound is combinatorial amortisation.	Structural analogue.
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	11	Fibonacci Heap Bounds	exact_linear	dynamical	Amortised bounds from a two-term potential function.	The potential counts trees plus twice the marked nodes — a linear functional of the structure’s state. Cascading cuts are paid for by the potential drop.	The potential is a linear functional of structural counts.	Exact linear (the potential).
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	12	Hash Table Load Factor	exact_linear	informational,geometric	A geometric series in the load factor.	The expected probe count is a Neumann series ∑𝛼𝑘, diverging as 𝛼→1. That divergence is why tables resize at a load factor well below one. The LKVexa Compendium — Calculus Edition 13	Probe count is a geometric series.	Exact linear.
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	13	Universal Hashing	exact_linear	structural	A family of hash functions with a pairwise independence guarantee.	The standard construction ℎ(𝑥) = ((𝑎𝑥+ 𝑏) mod 𝑝) mod 𝑚is an aﬀine map over a finite field, so universality is a property of aﬀine maps over 𝔽𝑝. Linear algebra over finite fields is doing the real work.	Universal hash families are aﬀine maps over a finite field. The LKVexa Compendium — Calculus Edition 14	Exact linear.
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	14	Birthday Bound	exact_linear	probabilistic,graph,combinatorial	A collision probability from pairwise counting.	The expected number of colliding pairs is (𝑛 2)/𝑚— linearity of expectation over pair indicators again. Collisions become likely at 𝑛≈√𝑚, which sets every hash length in cryptography.	The bound follows from linearity of expectation over pairs.	Exact linear.
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	15	Balanced Tree Height	exact_linear	dynamical	A logarithmic depth from bounded branching.	A tree with branching factor 𝑏and 𝑛nodes has depth log𝑏𝑛— the same reciprocal-of-contraction structure as binary search. Rebalancing rotations preserve the invariant that keeps this bound.	Depth is a log of node count.	Exact linear in logs.
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	16	B-Tree Height	exact_linear	dynamical	Depth with a large branching factor.	Increasing 𝑏from 2 to hundreds divides the depth by log2 𝑏— a linear rescaling of the logarithm. That rescaling is why disk-based indexes use wide nodes.	Branching factor rescales the log linearly.	Exact linear in logs.
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	17	Skip List Expected Height	structural_analogue	geometric	An expected maximum of geometric random variables.	Each element’s level is geometric, and the maximum of 𝑛such variables concentrates around log1/𝑝𝑛. The analysis is probabilistic rather than linear, achieving balanced-tree behaviour without rotations.	The height is a maximum of geometric variables.	Structural analogue.
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	18	Cache-Oblivious Complexity	structural_analogue	variational,dynamical,combinatorial,geometric	A memory transfer count in a two-level model.	The bound is optimal without knowing 𝐵or 𝑀, achieved by recursive divide-and-conquer that is self-similar at all scales. Its optimality is a statement about the recursion matching the memory hierarchy’s geometric structure.	Optimality follows from scale-invariant recursion.	Structural analogue.
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	19	Complexity Class P	structural_analogue	dynamical,combinatorial	A union of time-bounded classes.	Closed under composition and complement, which makes it robust across machine models. Closure properties are set-theoretic rather than linear.	Class definitions are set-theoretic.	Structural analogue.
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	20	Complexity Class NP	structural_analogue	combinatorial	An existential quantifier over polynomial-length certificates.	The asymmetry between existential and universal quantification is the whole content — co-NP swaps the quantifier. Quantifier structure is logical, not linear.	The class is defined by quantifier structure.	Structural analogue.
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	21	Polynomial-Time Reduction	structural_analogue	control	A transitive preorder on problems.	Composition of reductions is transitive, so the reduction relation is a preorder — completeness means being maximal in it. Composition of maps is the only algebra present, and it lacks additive structure.	Reductions compose; the structure is a preorder, not a vector space.	Structural analogue.
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	22	Cook–Levin Theorem	no_faithful_form	probabilistic,informational,combinatorial	An encoding of computation as a Boolean formula.	The proof builds a formula whose satisfying assignments are exactly accepting computation tableaux — a combinatorial encoding. Over 𝔽2, XOR-SAT is linear algebra and is solvable in polynomial time by Gaussian elimination; the hardness comes precisely from allowing OR, which breaks linearity. That contrast is the sharpest available statement of what linearity buys.	Linear (XOR) constraints are easy; the theorem’s hardness needs nonlinearity.	No faithful linear form — and the reason is instructive.
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	23	Space Hierarchy and Savitch’s Theorem	exact_linear	operator,graph	A recursive reachability argument on a configuration graph.	Savitch’s proof is repeated squaring of the reachability relation — which over the Boolean semiring is exactly matrix squaring on the configuration graph’s adjacency matrix. Reachability in 2𝑘steps is the 2𝑘th Boolean matrix power.	Savitch’s argument is Boolean matrix squaring.	Exact linear (Boolean semiring).
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	24	Time Hierarchy Theorem	no_faithful_form	structural	A diagonalisation argument.	Constructs a machine differing from every machine in the smaller class — Cantor’s diagonal argument applied to computations. Diagonalisation is a self-reference construction with no linear analogue.	Diagonalisation has no linear counterpart.	No faithful linear form.
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	25	Halting Problem Undecidability	no_faithful_form	operator,combinatorial	A self-referential contradiction.	The proof assumes a decider and builds a machine that contradicts it on its own description. Self- reference is the mechanism, and no matrix formulation captures it — attempting one would misrepresent the argument entirely.	Self-reference resists linear formulation. The LKVexa Compendium — Calculus Edition 25	No faithful linear form.
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	26	Rice’s Theorem	no_faithful_form	combinatorial	A sweeping generalisation of entry 25.	Reduces the halting problem to any non-trivial semantic property, so the undecidability is inherited by reduction. The obstruction is the same self-reference.	The theorem inherits the halting problem’s obstruction.	No faithful linear form.
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	27	Kolmogorov Complexity	no_faithful_form	combinatorial	A minimum description length — an uncomputable functional.	It is subadditive, 𝐾(𝑥𝑦) ≤𝐾(𝑥) + 𝐾(𝑦) + 𝑂(log), which is the closest thing to linearity available. Its uncomputability follows from the same self-reference as the halting problem.	Only subadditivity survives; the quantity is uncomputable.	No faithful linear form.
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	28	Invariance Theorem	exact_linear	structural	Machine-independence up to an additive constant.	Different universal machines differ by a bounded additive term — an equivalence up to translation. That additive structure is what makes the quantity meaningful at all.	Invariance is an additive-constant equivalence.	Exact linear (up to constants).
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	29	Approximation Ratio	exact_linear	exact_numeric	A worst-case ratio over instances.	Many approximation algorithms are analysed by LP relaxation and rounding — solving a linear program over a polytope, then rounding to an integer point. The integrality gap is the ratio between the LP and integer optima, so approximability is bounded by a linear program’s slack.	Approximation bounds often come from LP relaxations and integrality gaps.	Exact linear (via LP relaxation).
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	30	PCP Theorem	exact_linear	informational,combinatorial	A probabilistically checkable proof system.	The proof uses low-degree polynomial encodings and linearity testing — checking whether a function is close to linear over 𝔽2 with 𝑂(1) queries. Linearity testing is literally at the heart of the theorem, which makes it the most linear-algebraic result in complexity theory.	The proof relies on linearity testing over finite fields.	Exact linear (the testing component).
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	31	Randomised Complexity Classes	exact_linear	probabilistic,combinatorial	Classes defined by acceptance probability thresholds.	Amplification by repetition uses Chernoff bounds — concentration of a sum of independent indicators, which is linearity of expectation plus a tail bound. The gap between 1/3 and 2/3 is arbitrary precisely because amplification is so effective.	Amplification rests on concentration of linear sums.	Exact linear (amplification).
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	32	Quantum Complexity BQP	exact_linear	operator,dynamical,combinatorial	A class defined by unitary circuits on a Hilbert space.	Quantum computation is linear algebra: states are unit vectors, gates are unitary matrices, and a computation is a product of unitaries applied to a vector. Measurement is the only non-unitary step, and it is a projection followed by renormalisation.	Quantum computation is a product of unitary matrices.	Exact linear. The most literally linear model of computation.
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	33	Grover Search Speedup	exact_linear	operator,parallel	A product of two reflection operators.	The Grover iterate is the composition of a reflection about the marked state and a reflection about the uniform superposition — and the product of two reflections is a rotation. The state rotates by a fixed angle per step in a two-dimensional invariant subspace, so 𝜋/4 √ 𝑁steps reach the target. The quadratic speedup is a statement about angles in a plane.	Grover’s algorithm is a rotation built from two reflections.	Exact linear.
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	34	Shor Factoring Complexity	exact_linear	spectral,operator,control,combinatorial	Period finding via the quantum Fourier transform.	The QFT is a unitary matrix — the discrete Fourier transform — implemented in 𝑂(𝑛2) gates, and it converts period finding into a peak-detection problem. Factoring reduces to finding an eigenvalue phase of a modular multiplication operator, so the whole algorithm is phase estimation.	Shor’s algorithm is eigenvalue phase estimation via a unitary Fourier transform.	Exact linear.
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	35	Graph Traversal Complexity	exact_linear	operator,graph,combinatorial	A sweep over the adjacency structure.	Cost is linear in the size of the adjacency matrix’s sparse representation — one visit per nonzero entry. BFS layers are exactly the sets reached by successive Boolean matrix powers.	Traversal touches each nonzero of the adjacency matrix once.	Exact linear.
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	36	Dijkstra’s Algorithm	exact_linear	operator	A greedy relaxation over edge weights.	Each relaxation is 𝑑𝑣←min(𝑑𝑣, 𝑑𝑢+ 𝑤𝑢𝑣) — exactly a min-plus inner product. The algorithm’s correctness requires non-negative weights, which is the condition that the min-plus operator is monotone in the right way.	Relaxation is a min-plus operation.	Exact linear (min-plus semiring).
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	37	Bellman–Ford Complexity	exact_linear	spectral,operator,combinatorial	𝑉−1 rounds of full relaxation.	Each round is a min-plus matrix–vector product d ←𝑊⊗d, so the algorithm computes 𝑊⊗(𝑉−1) applied to the source indicator. Negative cycles are detected because the iteration fails to converge — a spectral- radius condition in the tropical semiring.	Bellman–Ford is repeated min-plus matrix–vector multiplication.	Exact linear (min-plus semiring).
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	38	Floyd–Warshall All-Pairs	exact_linear	operator,graph,combinatorial	A min-plus matrix “cube” computed in place.	The algorithm computes the min-plus closure 𝑊∗= 𝐼⊕𝑊⊕𝑊⊗2 ⊕⋯, and its triple loop is exactly the structure of matrix multiplication. The 𝑂(𝑉3) cost is the cost of one matrix multiply, which is why the two problems have the same complexity signature.	All-pairs shortest paths is min-plus matrix closure.	Exact linear (min-plus semiring).
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	39	A* Search Admissibility	exact_linear	operator,geometric	A lower-bound condition on a heuristic.	Consistency, ℎ(𝑛) ≤𝑐(𝑛, 𝑛′)+ℎ(𝑛′), is a triangle inequality — so a consistent heuristic is a metric-like potential. A* with a consistent heuristic is Dijkstra on reweighted edges 𝑤′ = 𝑤+ℎ(𝑛′)−ℎ(𝑛), an exact potential transformation.	A* is Dijkstra with a potential-transformed weight matrix.	Exact linear.
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	40	Minimum Spanning Tree Cut Property	exact_linear	operator,variational,graph	A matroid exchange property.	Spanning trees form the bases of a graphic matroid, and greedy optimality is equivalent to the matroid structure. Matroids generalise linear independence — the graphic matroid’s independent sets are exactly the linearly independent columns of the incidence matrix over 𝔽2.	MSTs are matroid bases; the matroid is linear independence over 𝔽2.	Exact linear.
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	41	Max-Flow Min-Cut	exact_linear	operator,variational,dynamical,combinatorial	A pair of linear programs in duality.	Exactly LP duality: the max-flow LP’s dual is the min-cut LP, and strong duality gives equality. Integrality follows because the constraint matrix is totally unimodular, so the LP optimum is automatically integral — one of the cleanest matrix-theoretic explanations of a combinatorial theorem.	The theorem is LP duality plus total unimodularity.	Exact linear.
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	42	Edmonds–Karp Complexity	exact_linear	operator,variational,dynamical,graph,combinatorial	BFS-based augmenting path selection.	Choosing shortest augmenting paths bounds the number of augmentations, giving a strongly polyno- mial bound. The residual graph is the constraint matrix’s current slack, so each augmentation is a pivot-like step.	Augmentation is a pivot on the flow polytope.	Exact linear.
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	43	Graph Colouring Bound	exact_linear	spectral,operator,graph	Bounds on the chromatic number.	The Hoffman bound 𝜒≥1 −𝜆max/𝜆min comes directly from the adjacency matrix’s spectrum, and the Lovász theta function sits between the clique and chromatic numbers as a semidefinite program. Spectral and SDP relaxations are the standard route to bounds here.	Colouring bounds come from the adjacency spectrum and SDP relaxations.	Exact linear (the bounds).
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	44	Strongly Connected Components	exact_linear	operator,graph	A block decomposition of the adjacency matrix. The LKVexa Compendium — Calculus Edition 43	Ordering vertices by component makes the adjacency matrix block triangular, and the components are the diagonal blocks — exactly the Frobenius normal form of a non-negative matrix. Irreducibility of a matrix is strong connectivity of its graph, which is what Perron–Frobenius requires.	SCCs are the diagonal blocks of the Frobenius normal form.	Exact linear.
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	45	RSA Encryption	exact_linear	structural	Exponentiation in a multiplicative group.	The group (ℤ/𝑛)∗is abelian, so exponentiation is a group endomorphism — linear in the exponent’s additive structure, since 𝑒and 𝑑are inverses mod 𝜙(𝑛). Security rests on factoring being hard, which is not a linear-algebraic obstruction.	Exponentiation is linear in the exponent group.	Exact linear in the exponent.
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	46	Diﬀie–Hellman Key Exchange	exact_linear	graph	Commutativity of exponentiation. The LKVexa Compendium — Calculus Edition 45	The scheme works because exponents commute — the map is bilinear in the exponent pair. That bilinearity is exactly what pairing-based cryptography exploits further.	The exchange is bilinear in the exponents.	Exact linear in the exponents.
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	47	Elliptic Curve Discrete Log	exact_linear	structural	Scalar multiplication in an abelian group.	The group law makes 𝑘𝑃a module action of ℤon the curve’s point group — linear in 𝑘. Smaller keys suﬀice because no sub-exponential algorithm is known, not because of any structural difference in the linearity. The LKVexa Compendium — Calculus Edition 46	Scalar multiplication is a module action.	Exact linear (as a module action).
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	48	Security Level in Bits	exact_linear	structural	A logarithmic measure of work.	Comparing key sizes across schemes is comparing logs of work factors, so security level is a linear scale in log space. Doubling key length does not double security for schemes with sub-exponential attacks — the map is not linear in key length.	Security level is linear in log work. The LKVexa Compendium — Calculus Edition 47	Exact linear in logs.
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	2	Structure: XOR is exactly vector addition over 𝔽2, and a uniform key makes the ciphertext uniform regardless	exact_linear	graph		XOR is exactly vector addition over 𝔽2, and a uniform key makes the ciphertext uniform regardless of the message — the translation acts transitively on the space. Perfect secrecy is a statement about a group acting simply transitively, which is as linear as cryptography gets.	The pad is translation in 𝔽𝑛 2 by a uniform element.	Exact linear. The LKVexa Compendium — Calculus Edition 48
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	50	Hash Function Properties	no_faithful_form	graph	Three increasingly strong one-wayness requirements.	A linear hash function would be trivially breakable — collisions are found by solving a linear system. Cryptographic hashes must therefore be deliberately far from linear, which makes this one of the few entries where linearity is the enemy.	Linearity would destroy the security property.	No faithful linear form, by design.
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	51	Merkle Tree Proof Size	exact_linear	graph	A path from a leaf to the root of a binary tree.	Verification recomputes hashes along a path, so proof size is the tree depth. It is the same logarithmic- depth structure as every balanced tree here.	Proof size is tree depth.	Exact linear in logs.
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	52	Amdahl’s Law	exact_linear	parallel	A speedup bounded by the serial fraction.	Total time is a sum of a fixed serial part and a divisible parallel part — a linear decomposition of the work vector. The bound 𝑆≤1/(1 −𝑝) is the limit as the parallel term vanishes.	The law is a linear work decomposition.	Exact linear.
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	53	Gustafson’s Law	exact_linear	dynamical,parallel	Speedup under a fixed time rather than fixed problem size.	Exactly linear in 𝑁, because the problem grows with the machine. The contrast with Amdahl is a contrast between two different linear models of what is held fixed.	Scaled speedup is aﬀine in processor count.	Exact linear.
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	54	Average Memory Access Time	exact_linear	structural	An aﬀine combination of hit and miss costs.	Exactly a weighted average, and multi-level hierarchies nest the same formula recursively — a chain of aﬀine maps. Each level’s miss rate weights the next level’s cost.	AMAT is a nested aﬀine combination.	Exact linear.
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	55	Cache Miss Categories	exact_linear	structural	An additive decomposition of miss counts.	Approximately additive, measured by comparing against idealised caches — a difference-based attri- bution. The decomposition is not orthogonal, so the categories overlap somewhat.	The categories are a difference-based additive attribution.	Exact linear.
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	56	Instruction Pipeline Speedup	exact_linear	parallel	A throughput ratio approaching the pipeline depth.	Fill and drain costs are an additive overhead, so speedup approaches 𝑘as 𝑛grows — an aﬀine correction to an ideal linear scaling. Hazards add further additive stall terms.	Pipelining is ideal scaling with an additive overhead.	Exact linear.
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	57	CPU Performance Equation	exact_linear	structural	A product of three factors — additive in logs.	Exactly multiplicative, so improvements in different factors compound. It separates the compiler’s, architecture’s, and technology’s contributions into independent multiplicands.	Performance is multiplicative, hence log-additive.	Exact linear in logs.
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	58	Little’s Law for Systems	exact_linear	probabilistic,dynamical	A stock–flow identity.	Exactly linear and distribution-free — it requires only that the system be stable, with no assumptions about arrival or service distributions. That generality is why it holds across queueing, epidemiology, and inventory alike.	The law is a linear stock–flow identity.	Exact linear.
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	59	Roofline Model	exact_linear	dynamical	A minimum of two linear bounds.	Performance is capped by the lower of a compute ceiling and a bandwidth-limited ramp, so the model is piecewise linear with a single kink. The kink’s location — the machine balance point — determines whether an algorithm is compute- or memory-bound.	The model is a minimum of two linear functions.	Exact linear (piecewise).
58	Computer Science — Calculus Edition	60	Shannon Entropy for Compression	exact_linear	informational,dynamical,learning	The expected code length lower bound.	Entropy is a linear functional of the surprisal vector, and the source coding theorem says no lossless code beats it on average. Huffman and arithmetic coding approach it, and the gap is at most one bit per symbol.	Entropy is a linear functional of surprisal.	Exact linear in surprisal.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	1	Sample Moments	exact_linear	operator,variational	A mean is a projection; a variance is a squared residual norm.	The mean is the projection of x onto the all-ones vector, ̄𝑥1 = 11⊤ 𝑛x, and the variance is the squared norm of the residual. The 𝑛−1 arises because that projection consumes one dimension — degrees of freedom is a rank statement.	Mean and variance are a projection and its residual norm.	Exact linear.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	2	Skewness and Kurtosis	exact_linear	probabilistic	Standardised third and fourth moments.	Higher moments are linear functionals of the distribution but nonlinear in the data, and standardising makes them scale-invariant. Their sample versions have high variance, which is why they are unreliable in small samples.	Moments are linear functionals of the distribution.	Exact linear.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	3	Quantile Definition	exact_linear	operator	A generalised inverse of a monotone function.	Sorting is a permutation matrix, and the quantile picks an entry of the permuted vector — so the operation is a permutation followed by a selection. Quantiles are equivariant under monotone transforms, which moments are not.	Quantiles are permutation-plus-selection.	Exact linear.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	4	Interquartile Range and Outlier Fence	exact_linear	structural	A linear contrast of two order statistics.	Exactly a difference and an aﬀine extension, so both are linear in the sorted vector. The 1.5 factor corresponds to about 0.7% tail mass under normality.	The fences are aﬀine in order statistics.	Exact linear.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	5	Median Absolute Deviation	exact_linear	variational	A robust scale estimate — a median of an ℓ1 residual.	The constant makes it consistent for the normal standard deviation, so it is a rescaled robust norm. It replaces the ℓ2 residual norm of the standard deviation with a median, trading eﬀiciency for a 50% breakdown point.	MAD replaces an ℓ2 norm with a median of absolute residuals.	Exact linear.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	6	Winsorised and Trimmed Mean	exact_linear	structural	A weighted mean with a truncated weight vector.	Exactly an inner product of the sorted vector with a weight vector that is zero at the ends — an L-estimator. The whole family of trimmed and Winsorised estimators is a family of weight vectors.	Trimmed means are inner products with truncated weight vectors.	Exact linear.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	7	Breakdown Point	exact_linear	structural	A robustness threshold.	The mean has breakdown zero because a single point moves it without bound — the weight vector is uniform and unbounded in influence. The median has breakdown 1/2, the maximum possible, because its weight vector concentrates on the centre.	Breakdown reflects the weight vector’s concentration.	Exact linear.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	8	Influence Function	exact_linear	probabilistic	A Gâteaux derivative of a statistical functional.	Exactly a directional derivative in the space of distributions, so it is the linearisation of an estimator. Bounded influence means the linearisation stays bounded, which is precisely what robustness requires.	The influence function is a functional derivative — a linearisation.	Exact linear (linearised).
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	9	Empirical CDF and Kolmogorov–Smirnov Distance	exact_linear	probabilistic,geometric	A sup-norm distance between two functions.	An ℓ∞distance in function space, so it is a norm — and its distribution-free null distribution comes from the Brownian bridge. The empirical CDF itself is a normalised sum of indicators, hence linear in the data.	The statistic is a sup-norm between two linear functionals.	Exact linear.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	10	Population Stability Index	exact_linear	operator,probabilistic,informational	A symmetrised KL-type divergence between two histograms.	A sum over bins of a product of a difference and a log ratio — linear in the bin contributions. It measures drift by comparing two probability vectors, and the binning choice is a projection onto a coarse basis.	PSI is an additive divergence over a binned basis.	Exact linear.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	11	Minkowski Distance	exact_linear	variational,geometric	The ℓ𝑝norm of a difference vector.	A genuine norm for 𝑝≥1 (the unit ball is convex), failing the triangle inequality below one. 𝑝= 1 gives Manhattan, 𝑝= 2 Euclidean, 𝑝→∞Chebyshev — a family of unit balls interpolating from a cross-polytope to a cube.	The distance is an ℓ𝑝norm of a difference.	Exact linear.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	12	Mahalanobis Distance	exact_linear	probabilistic,geometric	A quadratic form with the inverse covariance as metric.	Exactly a Euclidean distance after whitening by Σ−1/2 — a change of basis that makes correlated data isotropic. It is the natural distance whenever the data have a covariance structure, and it appears in discriminant analysis, outlier detection, and 𝑑′ alike.	Mahalanobis distance is Euclidean distance in whitened coordinates.	Exact linear.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	13	Cosine Similarity	exact_linear	structural	A normalised inner product. The LKVexa Compendium — Calculus Edition 14	Exactly the inner product on the unit sphere, so it measures angle and ignores magnitude. On centred data it equals the Pearson correlation — the same object under a different name.	Cosine similarity is a normalised inner product.	Exact linear.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	14	Jaccard Index	exact_linear	structural	A normalised inner product of binary vectors.	The intersection is a⊤b and the union is ‖a‖1 + ‖b‖1 −a⊤b, so the index is a ratio of linear forms. MinHash estimates it by sampling, preserving the ratio in expectation.	The index is a ratio of inner products and ℓ1 norms. The LKVexa Compendium — Calculus Edition 15	Exact linear.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	15	Edit (Levenshtein) Distance	exact_linear	operator,geometric	A dynamic programming matrix in the min-plus semiring.	The same tropical recursion as sequence alignment, and it satisfies the triangle inequality — so it is a genuine metric on strings. The metric property is what allows metric-tree indexing.	Edit distance is a min-plus recursion and a metric.	Exact linear (tropical semiring).
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	16	Earth Mover’s (Wasserstein) Distance	exact_linear	operator,variational,geometric	An optimal transport linear program.	Exactly a transportation LP with marginal constraints, and its dual is the Kantorovich–Rubinstein formulation over 1-Lipschitz functions. The constraint matrix is totally unimodular, so the optimum is achieved at a vertex with integral structure.	Wasserstein distance is a linear program with a totally unimodular constraint matrix.	Exact linear.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	17	Kullback–Leibler Divergence	exact_linear	variational,informational,dynamical,geometric	An expectation of a log ratio — linear in 𝑃.	Linear in the first argument given the log-ratio vector, but not symmetric and not a metric. Its asymmetry is exactly why forward and reverse KL give different variational approximations — mode-covering versus mode-seeking.	KL is a linear functional of 𝑃against a log-ratio vector.	Exact linear in 𝑃.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	18	Jensen–Shannon Divergence	exact_linear	variational,probabilistic,informational,geometric	A symmetrised divergence about the midpoint distribution.	The midpoint is a convex combination — a linear average — and the symmetrisation makes √ JSD a true metric. It is bounded by log 2, which makes it comparable across problems.	Symmetrisation about a linear midpoint yields a metric.	Exact linear.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	19	Locality-Sensitive Hashing	exact_linear	operator,probabilistic	A random projection followed by quantisation.	For cosine similarity the hash is sign(r⊤x) with r random Gaussian — a random hyperplane, so the collision probability is 1 −𝜃/𝜋. The whole scheme is a random linear map plus a sign, which is why it preserves angles.	LSH is a random linear projection plus quantisation.	Exact linear.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	20	Curse of Dimensionality	exact_linear	geometric	Concentration of distances in high dimension.	Distances concentrate because the sum of 𝑝independent contributions has relative fluctuation 𝑂(1/√𝑝) — a central limit statement. Nearest neighbours become meaningless, which is why every method in the next section reduces dimension first.	Distance concentration follows from summing many independent terms.	Exact linear.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	21	Principal Component Analysis	exact_linear	spectral,operator,probabilistic,geometric	The eigendecomposition of the covariance matrix.	Literally an eigenvalue problem: the components are eigenvectors, ordered by eigenvalue, and they are orthogonal because Σ is symmetric. Each successive component maximises a Rayleigh quotient subject to orthogonality with the previous ones.	PCA is the covariance matrix’s eigendecomposition.	Exact linear. Already native.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	22	Explained Variance Ratio	exact_linear	spectral	A normalised eigenvalue.	The trace of Σ is the total variance, so the eigenvalues partition it exactly — a spectral decomposition of variance. The cumulative sum’s shape is the scree plot.	Eigenvalues partition the trace.	Exact linear.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	23	PCA via SVD	exact_linear	spectral,operator,variational,probabilistic	The singular value decomposition of the centred data matrix.	𝑉’s columns are the principal directions and 𝜎2 𝑖/(𝑛−1) are the eigenvalues, so the SVD gives PCA without forming the covariance — which squares the condition number. Eckart–Young says the rank-𝑘truncation is the best rank-𝑘approximation in Frobenius norm, which is the whole justification for truncating.	PCA is the SVD; truncation is optimal by Eckart–Young.	Exact linear.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	24	Kernel PCA	exact_linear	operator	An eigendecomposition of the centred Gram matrix.	PCA in a feature space accessed only through inner products, so the computation is an 𝑛× 𝑛eigen- problem rather than a 𝑝×𝑝one. The kernel trick works precisely because PCA depends on the data only through inner products.	Kernel PCA is PCA on the Gram matrix.	Exact linear (in feature space).
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	25	Multidimensional Scaling	exact_linear	spectral,operator,control,geometric	A double-centred squared distance matrix, then eigendecomposed.	Double centring converts squared distances into inner products exactly, so classical MDS is PCA on the recovered Gram matrix. If 𝐷is Euclidean, 𝐵is positive semidefinite and the embedding is exact — negative eigenvalues measure non-Euclideanness.	MDS is an eigendecomposition of a double-centred distance matrix.	Exact linear.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	26	Isomap Geodesic Distance	exact_linear	spectral,operator,dynamical,graph,control,geometric	MDS applied to graph shortest-path distances.	The shortest paths are computed by min-plus matrix closure, then classical MDS eigendecomposes the result — a tropical computation feeding a spectral one. It unfolds a manifold exactly when the neighbourhood graph approximates geodesics well.	Isomap composes a min-plus closure with an eigendecomposition.	Exact linear.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	27	t-SNE Objective	no_faithful_form	variational,probabilistic,informational,geometric	A KL divergence between two neighbour distributions.	Minimised by gradient descent over embedding coordinates, so there is no closed form and no eigen- decomposition. The heavy-tailed low-dimensional kernel breaks any linear correspondence — distances between clusters in a t-SNE plot are not interpretable, which is a direct consequence of the objective being purely local.	The embedding is a nonconvex optimisation with no linear structure.	No faithful linear form. Global geometry is not preserved.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	28	UMAP Cross-Entropy Objective	no_faithful_form	spectral,variational,informational,graph	A fuzzy set cross-entropy between two graph representations.	Better global structure than t-SNE because the repulsive term penalises distant points that should be distant, but still a nonconvex graph layout problem. Its spectral initialisation is a Laplacian eigenmap, so the linear part is only the starting point.	Only the spectral initialisation is linear.	No faithful linear form (except the initialisation).
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	29	Random Projection Bound	exact_linear	operator,probabilistic,geometric	The Johnson–Lindenstrauss lemma.	A random linear map into 𝑘dimensions preserves all pairwise distances within (1 ± 𝜀) with high probability, and 𝑘is independent of the original dimension. It is a purely linear-algebraic result — a random matrix acts as a near-isometry on any fixed finite set.	A random linear map is a near-isometry on finite sets.	Exact linear.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	30	Non-Negative Matrix Factorisation	exact_linear	operator,variational	A constrained low-rank matrix factorisation.	Non-negativity forces a parts-based rather than a cancelling representation, and it makes the problem nonconvex — though convex in 𝑊and 𝐻separately, which is why alternating least squares works. Unlike the SVD the factorisation is not unique, so interpretation requires care.	NMF is a biconvex low-rank factorisation.	Exact linear (alternating).
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	31	K-Means Objective	exact_linear	spectral,operator	A total within-cluster scatter — a trace.	Minimising 𝐽is equivalent to maximising the between-cluster scatter, since the total is fixed — an exact trace decomposition, tr(𝑆𝑇) = tr(𝑆𝑊) + tr(𝑆𝐵). Relaxing the cluster indicator matrix to be orthonormal turns k-means into a PCA-like eigenproblem, which is the link to spectral methods.	K-means is a trace decomposition; its relaxation is an eigenproblem.	Exact linear (relaxed).
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	32	Silhouette Coeﬀicient	exact_linear	operator,geometric	A normalised contrast between within- and between-cluster distances.	Exactly a difference of two averages over the distance matrix, normalised — a linear functional per point. Averaging over points gives a single quality score.	The coeﬀicient is a normalised distance contrast.	Exact linear.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	33	Elbow and Gap Statistic	exact_linear	structural	A comparison of within-cluster dispersion against a null reference.	Exactly a contrast in log space between observed and reference dispersion, with the reference generated from a uniform null. It formalises the elbow by giving it a baseline.	The statistic is a log-space contrast against a null.	Exact linear in logs.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	34	Gaussian Mixture Model	exact_linear	operator,variational,probabilistic,dynamical,geometric	A convex combination of Gaussian densities.	Linear in the mixture weights, and each component’s covariance matrix defines a Mahalanobis metric — so the model is a set of ellipsoids. K-means is its limit as all covariances become 𝜀𝐼.	The mixture is a linear combination of densities.	Exact linear in the weights.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	35	EM Algorithm Steps	exact_linear	operator,variational,probabilistic,control	An alternation between a responsibility matrix and weighted estimates.	The E-step normalises rows of a weight matrix; the M-step computes weighted means and covariances — both linear operations. Monotone likelihood increase follows from a variational bound, and convergence is to a local optimum.	EM alternates two linear estimation steps.	Exact linear.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	36	DBSCAN Density Criterion	exact_linear	operator,graph,parallel,geometric	A thresholded row sum of the 𝜀-neighbourhood adjacency matrix.	Core points are those whose adjacency row sum exceeds the threshold, and clusters are connected components of the core-point subgraph. It is a graph connectivity computation on a thresholded distance matrix.	DBSCAN thresholds an adjacency matrix and finds components.	Exact linear.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	37	Hierarchical Linkage Criteria	exact_linear	operator,geometric	Different reduction operators on the distance matrix.	The Lance–Williams formula expresses every linkage as a linear recurrence on the merged distances, with different coeﬀicients per method. That single recurrence unifies all of them as one parametrised linear update. The LKVexa Compendium — Calculus Edition 37	All linkages are one linear recurrence with different coeﬀicients.	Exact linear.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	38	Spectral Clustering	exact_linear	spectral,variational,graph,combinatorial,geometric	The graph Laplacian’s smallest eigenvectors.	𝐿is symmetric positive semidefinite, and the multiplicity of eigenvalue zero equals the number of connected components — an exact rank statement. The Fiedler vector (second smallest) gives the optimal relaxed bipartition, so spectral clustering is a relaxation of a combinatorial cut problem into an eigenproblem.	Spectral clustering is an eigendecomposition of the Laplacian. The LKVexa Compendium — Calculus Edition 38	Exact linear.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	39	Adjusted Rand Index	exact_linear	dynamical	A chance-corrected agreement on a contingency table.	Built from pair counts in the contingency table between two clusterings, so it is a quadratic functional of the table. The chance correction subtracts the expectation under fixed margins — the rank-one baseline again.	ARI compares a table against a rank-one chance model.	Exact linear.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	40	Normalised Mutual Information	exact_linear	informational	A normalised divergence from the rank-one joint.	Mutual information measures departure of the joint contingency table from the outer product of its margins, normalised to [0, 1]. It is invariant to label permutation because permutations act on both margins consistently.	NMI is a normalised rank-one departure measure.	Exact linear.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	41	Autocorrelation Function	exact_linear	operator,probabilistic	The normalised diagonals of a Toeplitz covariance matrix.	Stationarity makes the covariance matrix Toeplitz — constant along diagonals — so the whole matrix is determined by one vector. Toeplitz systems are solved in 𝑂(𝑛2) by Levinson–Durbin rather than 𝑂(𝑛3), which is the structural payoff.	Stationary covariance is Toeplitz; the ACF is its first row.	Exact linear.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	42	Stationarity Conditions	exact_linear	spectral,operator	An eigenvalue containment condition.	Writing the AR process in companion-matrix form, stationarity is exactly the requirement that the companion matrix’s spectral radius is below one. It is the same condition as stability of a linear dynamical system.	Stationarity is a spectral radius condition on the companion matrix.	Exact linear.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	43	ARIMA Model	exact_linear	spectral,operator,dynamical	A ratio of two polynomial operators in the lag operator.	The backshift operator 𝐵is a shift matrix, and the polynomials in it are banded Toeplitz operators — so the model is a rational filter. Differencing (1 −𝐵)𝑑removes unit roots by cancelling eigenvalues at one.	ARIMA is a rational filter built from shift operators.	Exact linear.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	44	Exponential Smoothing	exact_linear	dynamical,geometric	A first-order linear recursion — an IIR filter.	Expanding gives geometrically decaying weights on past observations, so it is an inner product with an exponential weight vector. It is equivalent to an ARIMA(0,1,1) model, which is why it works well on trending series.	Smoothing is an exponentially weighted inner product.	Exact linear.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	45	STL Decomposition	exact_linear	operator,variational,learning	An additive decomposition into three components.	Each component is extracted by local regression (LOESS), which is a linear smoother — so the whole decomposition is a sum of three linear operators applied to the series. The operators are near-projections onto trend, seasonal, and residual subspaces.	STL applies three linear smoothers summing to the identity.	Exact linear.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	46	Dynamic Time Warping	exact_linear	operator,variational,graph,geometric	A min-plus dynamic programming matrix.	The same tropical recursion as edit distance and sequence alignment, computing an optimal monotone alignment path. It violates the triangle inequality, so it is not a metric — which complicates indexing.	DTW is a min-plus recursion.	Exact linear (tropical semiring).
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	47	CUSUM Change Detection	exact_linear	operator,variational,probabilistic	A rectified cumulative sum.	Cumulative summation is a triangular linear operator, and the rectifier resets it — so the statistic is a linear accumulation with a floor. It is optimal for detecting a step change in mean by the sequential probability ratio argument.	CUSUM is a rectified triangular accumulation.	Exact linear.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	48	EWMA Control Chart	exact_linear	control	The same exponential filter as entry 44, with control limits.	The variance of 𝑧𝑡converges to 𝜎2𝜆/(2−𝜆), computable exactly because the filter is linear. The limits widen to their asymptote as the filter reaches steady state.	The chart is a linear filter with a closed-form variance.	Exact linear.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	49	Isolation Forest Anomaly Score	structural_analogue	graph	A score from expected path lengths in random trees.	Anomalies are isolated by fewer random axis-aligned splits, so the score is a normalised expected depth. The random splits are axis-aligned, which means the method is sensitive to rotation — a genuine limitation of using coordinate-wise cuts on correlated data.	The score is a normalised expected depth; axis alignment breaks rotation invariance.	Structural analogue.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	50	Local Outlier Factor	exact_linear	operator,geometric	A ratio of local densities averaged over neighbours.	Local reachability density is a reciprocal of an average distance, so LOF is a ratio of two linear functionals of the distance matrix. It detects outliers relative to local density rather than globally.	LOF is a ratio of averaged distance functionals.	Exact linear.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	51	A/B Test Sample Size	exact_linear	structural	An inverse-square relation between sample and effect size.	The same Fisher information inversion as in trial design — required information scales as the inverse squared effect. Detecting a 1% lift needs roughly a hundred times the sample of a 10% lift.	Sample size inverts an information requirement.	Exact linear.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	52	CUPED Variance Reduction	exact_linear	operator,variational,learning	A regression adjustment using a pre-experiment covariate.	Exactly the least squares coeﬀicient, so the adjustment projects out the component of 𝑌explained by 𝑋— the residual has variance (1 −𝜌2)Var(𝑌). It is ANCOVA, and the variance reduction is the regression 𝑅2.	CUPED is a regression projection; variance reduction is 𝑅2.	Exact linear.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	53	Sequential Testing Always-Valid p-Value	exact_linear	probabilistic	A reciprocal of a running likelihood ratio.	The likelihood ratio is a non-negative martingale, so Ville’s inequality bounds the probability it ever exceeds 1/𝛼— validity at all stopping times follows from one martingale inequality. Log-likelihood ratios add over observations, which is what makes the running product tractable.	Validity follows from a martingale bound on an additive log-likelihood.	Exact linear in logs.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	54	Sample Ratio Mismatch Test	exact_linear	variational	A quadratic form in the observed-minus-expected vector.	Exactly a weighted squared norm of the residual, with weights 1/𝐸𝑖. A significant result invalidates the randomisation, so it is a check on the orthogonality that the experiment depends on.	The test is a weighted squared residual norm.	Exact linear.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	55	Simpson’s Paradox	no_faithful_form	structural	A failure of ratio comparisons to survive aggregation.	Ratios are not linear functionals, so their ordering is not preserved under addition of numerators and denominators — the mediant of two fractions need not lie in the same order. The paradox is precisely a demonstration that ratios do not add.	Ratios are not linear; aggregation does not preserve their order.	No faithful linear form. The paradox is the failure of linearity.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	56	Precision, Recall, and F1	exact_linear	operator,geometric	Row and column normalisations of a confusion matrix, and their harmonic mean. The LKVexa Compendium — Calculus Edition 55	Precision normalises by the predicted column, recall by the actual row — the same transposition structure as PPV and sensitivity. The harmonic mean penalises imbalance, which is why 𝐹1 is low unless both are high.	The metrics are row and column normalisations of one matrix.	Exact linear.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	57	Precision–Recall AUC	exact_linear	structural	A Riemann sum — a linear functional of the curve. The LKVexa Compendium — Calculus Edition 56	Exactly a weighted sum of precisions with recall increments as weights. Unlike ROC AUC it depends on class balance, since the baseline is the positive rate rather than 0.5.	Average precision is a weighted sum along the curve.	Exact linear.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	58	Normalised Discounted Cumulative Gain	exact_linear	variational	An inner product of gains with a positional discount vector.	Exactly a weighted sum where the weight vector is a fixed logarithmic discount, normalised by the ideal ordering. Sorting is a permutation, so nDCG measures how close the achieved permutation is to the optimal one under a fixed weight vector. The LKVexa Compendium — Calculus Edition 57	nDCG is a normalised inner product with a discount vector.	Exact linear.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	59	Bootstrap Confidence Interval	exact_linear	probabilistic,exact_numeric	Quantiles of a resampling distribution.	Resampling multiplies the data by a multinomial weight vector, so each bootstrap replicate is a reweighted statistic. For linear statistics the bootstrap distribution has an exact mean and variance, which is why it works so well for means and so poorly for extremes.	Bootstrapping is random reweighting.	Exact linear.
59	Data Science — Calculus Edition	60	Benford’s Law	exact_linear	probabilistic	A scale-invariant distribution over leading digits.	The only distribution invariant under multiplication by any constant, because taking logs turns scaling into translation and the uniform distribution on the circle is translation-invariant. It is a Haar measure argument, and its use in fraud detection is a goodness-of-fit test against this fixed vector.	Benford’s law is translation invariance in log space.	Exact linear in logs.
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	1	Empirical Risk Minimisation	exact_linear	variational,learning	An average loss over a hypothesis class.	The empirical risk is a linear functional of the per-example loss vector, so the objective is a mean — and its concentration around the true risk is the whole content of generalisation theory. For linear 𝑓and convex 𝐿the problem is convex.	Empirical risk is a mean of a loss vector.	Exact linear.
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	2	Bias–Variance Decomposition	exact_linear	operator	An orthogonal decomposition of squared error.	The cross terms vanish exactly because the bias is the deviation of the mean and the variance is the deviation from it — Pythagoras in the space of random variables. It is the same orthogonal split as in ANOVA and in the Brier score.	The decomposition is an orthogonal projection identity.	Exact linear.
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	3	Double Descent	exact_linear	spectral,operator,exact_numeric	A conditioning catastrophe at 𝑝= 𝑛.	When parameters equal samples the design matrix is square and typically ill-conditioned, so the minimum-norm solution has enormous norm — the smallest singular value approaches zero. Beyond the threshold, extra parameters give the pseudoinverse more room and the minimum-norm solution becomes better behaved. Double descent is a statement about the smallest singular value of 𝑋.	The peak is where 𝑋’s smallest singular value is worst.	Exact linear.
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	4	Squared Error Loss	exact_linear	operator,variational,dynamical,learning	A quadratic form in the residual.	Its minimiser is the conditional mean, and for linear models the solution is a projection onto the column space — the normal equations. Quadratic loss is the unique loss whose optimum is the mean.	Squared loss gives a projection solution.	Exact linear.
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	5	Absolute and Huber Loss	exact_linear	learning	A piecewise objective, quadratic near zero and linear in the tails.	Absolute loss gives the conditional median rather than the mean, and Huber interpolates — bounded influence in the tails, so outliers pull with constant rather than growing force. It is exactly a bounded influence function.	Huber bounds the influence function; the quadratic region stays linear.	Exact linear (piecewise).
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	6	Cross-Entropy Loss	exact_linear	variational,probabilistic,informational,learning	An inner product of a one-hot vector with a log-probability vector.	Exactly a linear functional of the log-probabilities, so gradients with respect to the logits are simply ̂p −y — remarkably clean, and the reason softmax and cross-entropy are always paired.	Cross-entropy is an inner product in log space; its logit gradient is a difference.	Exact linear in logs.
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	7	Softmax Function	exact_linear	control	A normalised exponential — a map from logits to the simplex.	Shift-invariant, so the logits have one redundant dimension — the map’s Jacobian diag(p) −pp⊤is singular with 1 in its null space. It is the same normalised weight vector as every partition function in this compendium.	Softmax is a normalised weight vector with a rank-deficient Jacobian.	Exact linear in the weights.
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	8	Hinge Loss	exact_linear	variational,learning	A rectified linear function of the margin.	Piecewise linear with a kink at margin one, so the subgradient is either zero or −𝑦x — only misclassified and margin points contribute. That sparsity in the gradient is what produces support vectors.	Hinge loss is a rectified linear function; its subgradient is sparse.	Exact linear (piecewise).
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	9	Focal Loss	exact_linear	informational,learning	Cross-entropy with a confidence-dependent reweighting.	The modulating factor down-weights easy examples, so it is a diagonal reweighting of the per-example loss vector that depends on the current prediction. It is an adaptive weight, not a change of the loss’s structure.	Focal loss is cross-entropy with an adaptive diagonal weight.	Exact linear in the weighting.
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	10	Regularised Objective	exact_linear	variational,dynamical,graph,learning	A sum of a data term and a penalty term.	Exactly additive, so the penalty shifts the optimum along a regularisation path parametrised by 𝜆. The Lagrangian view makes it a constrained problem — the penalty is the dual of a norm ball constraint.	Regularisation adds a penalty; the path is a parametrised family of solutions.	Exact linear.
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	11	Ridge and Lasso Penalties	exact_linear	spectral,geometric,exact_numeric	Two norms whose unit balls have different geometry. The LKVexa Compendium — Calculus Edition 12	Ridge has a closed form ̂𝜃= (𝑋⊤𝑋+ 𝜆𝐼)−1𝑋⊤y — the penalty shifts every eigenvalue of 𝑋⊤𝑋up by 𝜆, which is exactly why it fixes ill-conditioning. Lasso’s corners on the ℓ1 ball make the solution sparse, since the optimum lands on a face where coordinates are exactly zero.	Ridge shifts the spectrum; lasso’s ball geometry produces sparsity.	Exact linear.
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	12	Elastic Net	exact_linear	variational	A convex combination of two norms.	The ℓ2 term makes the objective strictly convex, so correlated predictors are selected together rather than arbitrarily — the grouping effect. It is a linear interpolation between two penalty geometries.	Elastic net linearly interpolates two norm balls.	Exact linear. The LKVexa Compendium — Calculus Edition 13
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	13	Generalisation Gap	exact_linear	structural	A deviation between a population and an empirical mean.	Bounding it uniformly over the hypothesis class is the core problem, and the bounds are concentration inequalities on sums of independent terms. Linearity of expectation plus a tail bound does all the work.	The gap is a concentration statement about a mean.	Exact linear. The LKVexa Compendium — Calculus Edition 14
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	14	VC Dimension Bound	exact_linear	informational,combinatorial	A capacity measure counting realisable labellings.	For linear classifiers in ℝ𝑝the VC dimension is exactly 𝑝+ 1 — the number of free parameters of a hyperplane. That equality is the cleanest link between a combinatorial capacity and a dimension.	For hyperplanes, VC dimension equals the parameter count.	Exact linear (linear classifiers). The LKVexa Compendium — Calculus Edition 15
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	15	Rademacher Complexity	exact_linear	combinatorial	An expected supremum of a random inner product.	Exactly the expected maximum correlation with random signs — an inner product of the function’s values with a random ±1 vector. For linear classes bounded in norm it evaluates in closed form via Cauchy– Schwarz, giving ℜ𝑛≤𝐵𝑅/√𝑛.	Rademacher complexity is an expected supremum of inner products.	Exact linear.
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	16	PAC Learning Sample Complexity	exact_linear	informational,learning,combinatorial	A sample requirement scaling with capacity.	Linear in the dimension and inverse-square in accuracy, which is the same information-theoretic scaling as everywhere else. Doubling accuracy needs four times the data.	Sample complexity is linear in capacity, inverse-square in accuracy.	Exact linear.
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	17	Margin Bound	exact_linear	informational,control,combinatorial	A bound depending on the margin rather than the dimension.	The complexity term involves ‖w‖ 2 𝑅2 rather than 𝑝, so it is dimension-free — which explains why SVMs and kernel methods work in infinite-dimensional spaces. Norm control replaces dimension control.	Norm-based capacity replaces dimension-based capacity.	Exact linear.
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	18	Bayes Error Rate	exact_linear	probabilistic,control	An irreducible error from class overlap.	A linear functional of the posterior, and no classifier can beat it. It is the analogue of the noise floor in every other estimation problem here.	The Bayes rate is a linear functional of the posterior.	Exact linear.
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	19	No Free Lunch for Learning	structural_analogue	learning,geometric	A symmetry argument over the space of target functions.	Averaging over a symmetric set of problems makes every algorithm’s expected performance identical — a group-invariance statement. It says inductive bias is necessary, not that any particular bias is good.	The theorem is a symmetry (invariance) argument.	Structural analogue.
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	20	Cross-Validation Estimate	exact_linear	operator,dynamical,learning	An average over held-out folds.	Exactly a mean of 𝐾estimates, though the folds are dependent — so the variance of the estimate is not the naive one. For linear models with squared loss, leave-one-out has the closed form ∑(𝑟𝑖/(1 −ℎ𝑖𝑖))2 where ℎ𝑖𝑖are the hat matrix’s diagonal entries, so no refitting is needed at all.	LOOCV for linear models is a closed form in the hat matrix’s diagonal.	Exact linear.
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	21	SVM Primal Problem	exact_linear	variational,geometric	A quadratic program with linear constraints.	Convex with a unique optimum, and maximising the margin is minimising the norm — the geometric margin is 2/ ‖w‖. It is a quadratic objective over a polyhedron.	The SVM is a quadratic program.	Exact linear (quadratic program).
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	22	SVM Dual Problem	exact_linear	operator,variational	A quadratic form in the dual variables built from a Gram matrix.	The data appear only through inner products, which is precisely what makes the kernel trick possible. Complementary slackness makes most 𝛼𝑖zero — the support vectors are the active constraints.	The dual is a quadratic form in the Gram matrix.	Exact linear.
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	23	Kernel Trick	exact_linear	operator	An inner product in an implicit feature space.	Mercer’s theorem says any positive semidefinite kernel corresponds to some feature map, so the Gram matrix’s positive semidefiniteness is the condition for a valid kernel. Computation in the feature space never happens — only the 𝑛× 𝑛matrix is formed.	Kernels are inner products; validity is positive semidefiniteness.	Exact linear.
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	24	Common Kernels	exact_linear	operator	Three positive semidefinite kernels with different feature spaces.	The RBF kernel’s feature space is infinite-dimensional, yet the Gram matrix is finite — which is the whole point. Sums and products of kernels are kernels, so the valid kernels form a cone closed under those operations.	Kernels form a closed cone; the RBF’s feature space is infinite-dimensional.	Exact linear.
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	25	Representer Theorem	exact_linear	operator	A finite expansion in kernel functions at the data points.	The optimum of any norm-regularised objective lies in the span of the training kernels — the component orthogonal to that span only increases the norm without changing the fit. It is a projection argument, and it reduces an infinite-dimensional optimisation to 𝑛coeﬀicients.	The theorem is an orthogonal projection argument.	Exact linear.
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	26	Gaussian Process Posterior	exact_linear	operator,probabilistic,dynamical,learning	A linear solve against a regularised kernel matrix.	The posterior mean is exactly kernel ridge regression, and the posterior covariance 𝐾∗∗−𝐾∗(𝐾+ 𝜎2𝐼)−1𝐾⊤ ∗is a Schur complement. The 𝑂(𝑛3) cost is the Cholesky factorisation, which is what limits GPs to moderate 𝑛.	GP regression is a linear solve; the variance is a Schur complement.	Exact linear.
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	27	Bagging Variance Reduction	exact_linear	structural	The variance of an average of correlated estimators.	Averaging 𝐵correlated predictors leaves an irreducible 𝜌𝜎2 floor, so the benefit is capped by the correlation. This formula is the entire justification for the next entry.	The variance of a mean of correlated variables has a correlation floor.	Exact linear.
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	28	Random Forest Feature Subsampling	exact_linear	variational	A device for lowering 𝜌in entry 27.	Restricting each split to a random feature subset decorrelates the trees, pushing 𝜌down so averaging helps more. It trades a small increase in individual tree bias for a large drop in the correlation floor.	Subsampling reduces the correlation term of the variance formula.	Exact linear.
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	29	Out-of-Bag Error	exact_linear	probabilistic	A built-in held-out estimate.	The probability a sample is omitted from a bootstrap draw is (1−1/𝑛)𝑛→𝑒−1, so about 37% of trees never saw each point. That gives cross-validation for free — an exact consequence of the resampling distribution.	The 𝑒−1 fraction follows from the bootstrap’s inclusion probability.	Exact linear.
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	30	AdaBoost Weight Update	exact_linear	probabilistic,dynamical,learning	A multiplicative reweighting of the sample distribution.	Multiplicative in weights means additive in log weights, and AdaBoost is exactly coordinate descent on the exponential loss. The learner weight 𝛼𝑡= 1 2 ln 1−𝜀𝑡 𝜀𝑡 is a log-odds — the same object as in every log-linear model here.	AdaBoost is coordinate descent on exponential loss; weights are log-additive.	Exact linear in logs.
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	31	Gradient Boosting	exact_linear	variational,dynamical	A functional gradient descent — an additive expansion.	The model is a sum of weak learners, each fitted to the negative gradient — so boosting is gradient descent in function space with a shrinkage step size 𝜈. Additivity is what makes the ensemble a linear combination of basis functions.	Boosting is gradient descent in function space; the model is an additive expansion.	Exact linear (the expansion).
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	32	XGBoost Regularised Objective	linearized	variational,learning	A second-order Taylor expansion of the loss plus a penalty.	Using both gradient and Hessian gives the optimal leaf weight in closed form, −𝐺𝑗/(𝐻𝑗+ 𝜆) — a Newton step on a diagonal Hessian. The split gain is the resulting objective improvement, which is a difference of such quadratic terms.	XGBoost takes Newton steps with a diagonal Hessian.	Linearised (second order).
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	33	Feedforward Layer	exact_linear	operator,graph,learning	An aﬀine map followed by a pointwise nonlinearity.	The aﬀine part is the entire learned content; without 𝜎a deep network collapses to a single matrix product, since the composition of aﬀine maps is aﬀine. Depth is only meaningful because of the nonlinearity — this is the single most important fact about neural networks stated in linear-algebraic terms.	A layer is an aﬀine map plus a nonlinearity; without the latter, depth is vacuous.	Exact linear apart from 𝜎.
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	34	Universal Approximation Theorem	exact_linear	structural	A density statement about a span of ridge functions.	The span of {𝜎(w⊤x + 𝑏)} is dense in continuous functions on compacts — a statement about a spanning set, not about how many elements are needed. The required width may be exponential, which is why the theorem justifies expressiveness but not practice.	The theorem is a density statement about a span.	Exact linear (as a span statement).
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	35	Backpropagation	exact_linear	operator,variational,dynamical,learning,control	A product of Jacobians, evaluated right to left.	The chain rule gives 𝜕𝐿/𝜕x = 𝐽⊤ 1 𝐽⊤ 2 ⋯𝐽⊤ 𝐿∇𝐿, and reverse-mode evaluation is cheap because a matrix– vector product costs far less than a matrix–matrix one. Each weight gradient is a rank-one outer product, which is why the update is eﬀicient. Backpropagation is an associativity choice in a matrix product.	Backpropagation is a Jacobian product, associated right to left.	Exact linear.
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	36	Activation Functions	exact_linear	graph,combinatorial	The pointwise nonlinearities that make depth meaningful.	ReLU is piecewise linear, so a ReLU network is a piecewise linear function whose pieces are polyhedral regions — the network partitions input space into cells and is aﬀine on each. Counting those regions bounds expressivity.	ReLU networks are piecewise aﬀine on a polyhedral partition.	Exact linear (piecewise).
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	37	Vanishing and Exploding Gradients	exact_linear	spectral,operator,variational,control	A product of many Jacobians.	The gradient norm is governed by the product of the Jacobians’ singular values, so anything other than a product near one is exponentially bad in depth. It is exactly the Lyapunov exponent of a matrix product — the same object as in random matrix products elsewhere in this compendium.	The problem is the Lyapunov exponent of a Jacobian product.	Exact linear.
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	38	Xavier and He Initialisation	exact_linear	spectral,control	A variance scaling chosen to keep Jacobian singular values near one.	The scaling preserves activation variance layer to layer, which keeps the Jacobian product’s spectral norm near unity. It is a direct engineering fix for entry 37, derived by a variance propagation calculation.	Initialisation tunes the Jacobian spectrum to preserve scale.	Exact linear.
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	39	Batch Normalisation	exact_linear	spectral,variational,informational,learning,exact_numeric	A per-feature standardisation followed by an aﬀine map. The LKVexa Compendium — Calculus Edition 39	Centring and scaling is a diagonal whitening of the activations, which improves the loss landscape’s conditioning — it makes the Hessian’s eigenvalues more uniform. The learned 𝛾, 𝛽restore representational capacity that the normalisation would otherwise remove.	Batch norm is a diagonal whitening plus an aﬀine map.	Exact linear.
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	40	Layer Normalisation	exact_linear	operator,control	The same whitening along the other axis.	Normalising per example makes the operation batch-independent, which is why transformers use it. It is the transpose of the batch norm operation in terms of which axis is reduced. The LKVexa Compendium — Calculus Edition 40	Layer norm whitens along the transposed axis.	Exact linear.
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	41	Dropout	exact_linear	operator,learning	A random diagonal mask applied to activations.	Exactly multiplication by a random diagonal matrix, and its expectation is a scalar multiple — which is why inference simply scales. For linear regression, dropout is provably equivalent to ℓ2 regularisation with a data-dependent scaling.	Dropout is a random diagonal matrix; in expectation it is ridge-like.	Exact linear. The LKVexa Compendium — Calculus Edition 41
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	42	Residual Connection	exact_linear	operator,variational,dynamical,graph,control	An identity plus a learned perturbation.	The Jacobian becomes 𝐼+ 𝜕𝐹/𝜕𝑥, so gradients flow through the identity path regardless of what 𝐹 does — the product of Jacobians is bounded away from zero. It is the cleanest fix to entry 37, and it works by adding an identity matrix.	Residuals add an identity to each Jacobian.	Exact linear.
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	43	Convolution Operation	exact_linear	operator	A Toeplitz (or block-circulant) matrix acting on the input.	Convolution is a linear map with tied, sparse weights — the same matrix structure as any translation- invariant operator, diagonalised by the Fourier transform. Weight sharing is exactly the constraint that the matrix be Toeplitz, and that constraint is where the parameter savings come from.	Convolution is multiplication by a Toeplitz matrix.	Exact linear.
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	44	Convolution Output Size	exact_linear	operator,control	The output dimension of the Toeplitz operator.	Padding and stride determine the matrix’s shape, so the formula is a dimension count. Stride subsam- ples the rows, which is a selection matrix composed with the convolution.	The formula counts the operator’s output dimension.	Exact linear.
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	45	Receptive Field Growth	exact_linear	operator,control	The support of a composed sequence of local operators.	Composing banded matrices widens the band additively for stride one and multiplicatively with striding — the support of the product grows as the sum of the supports. Dilated convolutions widen the band without adding parameters.	The receptive field is the support of a product of banded matrices.	Exact linear.
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	46	Scaled Dot-Product Attention	exact_linear	operator,variational,probabilistic	Three linear projections, a Gram matrix, a softmax, and a weighted sum.	𝑄= 𝑋𝑊𝑄, 𝐾= 𝑋𝑊𝐾, 𝑉= 𝑋𝑊𝑉are all linear; 𝑄𝐾⊤is a similarity Gram matrix; the softmax makes it row-stochastic; multiplying by 𝑉is a convex combination of value vectors. The scaling by √𝑑𝑘keeps the logits’ variance constant so the softmax does not saturate. Everything but one softmax is matrix multiplication.	Attention is a data-dependent row-stochastic matrix applied to a linear projection.	Exact linear apart from the softmax.
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	47	Multi-Head Attention	exact_linear	operator,informational	A block-diagonal collection of low-rank projections, recombined linearly.	Each head projects to 𝑑/ℎdimensions, so each is a rank-limited attention operation, and concatenation plus 𝑊𝑂is a linear recombination. The total parameter count matches single-head attention at full width — heads buy diversity of subspaces, not capacity.	Multi-head attention is a block-structured low-rank decomposition.	Exact linear apart from the softmaxes.
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	48	Positional Encoding	exact_linear	informational	A fixed Fourier basis added to the embeddings.	Because relative positions correspond to rotations in each frequency’s two-dimensional subspace, a shift is a linear map on the encoding — which is what lets attention learn relative offsets. Rotary embeddings make this explicit by applying the rotation directly to 𝑄and 𝐾.	Positional encoding is a Fourier basis; shifts act as rotations.	Exact linear.
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	49	Transformer Parameter Count	exact_linear	operator	A count dominated by the four 𝑑× 𝑑weight matrices per layer.	Attention contributes 4𝑑2 (Q, K, V, O) and the feedforward block 8𝑑2 at the usual 4× width — so parameters scale quadratically in width and linearly in depth. Every parameter is an entry of a matrix.	The count is the total size of the layer matrices.	Exact linear.
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	50	Neural Scaling Law	exact_linear	learning	A power law — linear in log–log.	Loss falls as a power of parameters, data, or compute, so fitting the exponents is a linear regression in log space. The predictability across many orders of magnitude is what makes large-scale training plannable.	Scaling laws are linear in logs.	Exact linear in logs.
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	51	Compute-Optimal Allocation	exact_linear	variational,learning	A constrained optimisation of a log-linear loss surface.	Minimising loss subject to a compute budget 𝐶≈6𝑁𝐷is a Lagrange problem on a log-linear surface, so the solution is a fixed ratio of exponents. The Chinchilla result — roughly 20 tokens per parameter — is the solution of that two-variable optimisation.	The allocation solves a Lagrange condition on a log-linear surface.	Exact linear in logs.
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	52	Autoencoder Reconstruction	exact_linear	variational,dynamical,learning	A composition of an encoder and a decoder.	With linear 𝑓and 𝑔and squared loss, the optimal autoencoder spans exactly the same subspace as PCA — the solution is the leading singular subspace, though not necessarily the individual components. Nonlinearity is what lets it beat PCA.	A linear autoencoder recovers the PCA subspace.	Exact linear (linear case).
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	53	Variational Autoencoder ELBO	exact_linear	variational,probabilistic,informational,dynamical	A reconstruction term minus a divergence term.	For Gaussian 𝑞and 𝑝the KL term has a closed form in the means and log-variances — a sum of simple scalar expressions per dimension, since the covariances are diagonal. The bound is exactly the log evidence minus the posterior approximation gap.	The Gaussian KL is a closed-form sum over diagonal components.	Exact linear (Gaussian case).
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	54	Reparametrisation Trick	exact_linear	variational,graph	An aﬀine map of a fixed noise variable.	Writing the sample as an aﬀine function of parameter-free noise makes the gradient pass through — the randomness is moved outside the differentiated path. It is precisely the linearity of the location–scale family that makes this work.	The trick exploits the aﬀine structure of location–scale families.	Exact linear.
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	55	GAN Minimax Objective	linearized	spectral,variational,informational,dynamical,control	A two-player zero-sum game.	At the optimal discriminator the objective reduces to the Jensen–Shannon divergence, but the game dynamics are a nonconvex-nonconcave saddle point problem. Linearising the joint dynamics gives a Jacobian with imaginary eigenvalues — which is exactly why training oscillates rather than converges.	Training instability is imaginary eigenvalues of the game’s Jacobian.	Linearised.
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	56	Wasserstein GAN	exact_linear	variational	A supremum of a linear functional over a Lipschitz ball.	Exactly the Kantorovich–Rubinstein dual of the transport LP — linear in the measures, with the constraint set being a norm ball in function space. The gradient penalty enforces the Lipschitz constraint approximately.	The objective is a linear program’s dual over a Lipschitz ball.	Exact linear.
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	57	Diffusion Forward Process	exact_linear	probabilistic,learning	An aﬀine interpolation between data and noise.	The forward process is exactly linear — a scalar times the data plus a scalar times Gaussian noise — with a closed form at every 𝑡because Gaussians are closed under aﬀine maps. All the learning happens in the reverse process.	The forward process is an aﬀine map with a closed form.	Exact linear.
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	58	Diffusion Training Objective	exact_linear	operator,variational,learning	A squared error on the noise vector.	Predicting the noise is equivalent to predicting the score up to a scalar, so this simple regression objective secretly learns the gradient of the log density. The whole training procedure is least squares on a linear target.	Training is least squares on an aﬀine-transformed target.	Exact linear.
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	59	Score Function and Langevin Sampling	exact_linear	spectral,variational,probabilistic	A gradient step plus noise — a discretised Langevin diffusion.	For a Gaussian target the score is exactly linear, −Σ−1(x −𝜇), so sampling is an Ornstein–Uhlenbeck process whose convergence rate is Σ’s smallest eigenvalue. Ill-conditioned targets mix slowly for exactly the reason ill-conditioned linear systems converge slowly.	For Gaussian targets Langevin sampling is a linear OU process.	Exact linear (Gaussian case).
60	Machine Learning — Calculus Edition	60	Contrastive Learning (InfoNCE)	exact_linear	operator,informational,learning	A softmax over inner products with a temperature.	The similarities are inner products of normalised embeddings — a Gram matrix — and the loss is cross-entropy over its rows. It lower-bounds the mutual information between views, and the temperature rescales the Gram matrix before the softmax.	InfoNCE is cross-entropy over a scaled Gram matrix.	Exact linear apart from the softmax.
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	1	Uninformed Search Complexity	structural_analogue	spectral,combinatorial,geometric	An exponential count over a branching tree.	The frontier size grows geometrically, so the cost is a geometric series dominated by its last term. It is counting rather than linear algebra, though the geometric growth is the same structure as a single-eigenvalue expansion.	Growth is geometric; the analysis is counting.	Structural analogue.
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	2	Iterative Deepening	exact_linear	dynamical,geometric	Repeated bounded searches whose costs form a geometric series.	Re-expanding shallow nodes costs only a constant factor because ∑𝑖≤𝑑𝑏𝑖≈𝑏𝑑⋅ 𝑏 𝑏−1 — the geometric series’ sum is dominated by its last term. Trading time for space is free up to that factor.	The overhead is a geometric series ratio.	Exact linear.
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	3	A* Evaluation Function	exact_linear	operator,graph	A sum of a path cost and a heuristic estimate.	Exactly additive, and the addition is what makes the priority queue ordering meaningful. With ℎa potential function, A* is Dijkstra on reweighted edges — a linear transformation of the weight matrix.	A* is Dijkstra under a potential transformation.	Exact linear.
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	4	Admissibility and Consistency	exact_linear	variational,geometric	A lower bound and a triangle inequality.	Consistency is exactly the triangle inequality, making ℎbehave like a metric potential — which guarantees the reweighted edge costs stay non-negative. Admissibility alone guarantees optimality but permits re-expansion.	Consistency is a triangle inequality on a potential.	Exact linear.
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	5	Heuristic Dominance	structural_analogue	dynamical	A partial order on heuristics.	Pointwise domination gives a pointwise ordering of expanded node sets — a monotone relation. The maximum of two admissible heuristics is admissible, so admissible heuristics form a lattice under max.	Heuristics form a lattice under pointwise max.	Structural analogue.
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	6	Effective Branching Factor	exact_linear	dynamical,geometric	The root of a geometric series matching the observed node count.	Solving for 𝑏∗inverts a geometric sum — a one-dimensional monotone solve. It is the standard summary of a heuristic’s practical quality.	The factor inverts a geometric series.	Exact linear.
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	7	Pattern Database Heuristic	exact_linear	structural	A combination of abstraction-based lower bounds.	Disjoint patterns allow addition because their costs partition the problem — an additive decomposition valid exactly when the abstractions do not share cost. Otherwise only the max is admissible.	Disjointness licenses addition; otherwise only max is safe.	Exact linear (disjoint case).
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	8	Minimax Value	structural_analogue	operator,graph	An alternating max-min recursion — a tropical recursion with two operators.	Alternating max and min over a game tree is a recursion in a semiring with two operations, so it does not reduce to a single tropical semiring. The alternation is what makes game trees fundamentally different from shortest-path problems.	Alternating max-min is not a single semiring recursion. The LKVexa Compendium — Calculus Edition 9	Structural analogue.
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	9	Alpha–Beta Pruning Complexity	exact_linear	combinatorial	An effective halving of the exponent.	Perfect move ordering halves the depth exponent, which is equivalent to a square root of the search space — the same quadratic improvement as Grover’s algorithm, though achieved by pruning rather than inter- ference. Move ordering quality determines where between 𝑏𝑑and 𝑏𝑑/2 the true cost falls.	Pruning halves the exponent.	Exact linear in logs.
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	10	Monte Carlo Tree Search UCT	exact_linear	structural	An exploitation term plus an exploration bonus.	Exactly the UCB1 formula, where the bonus is a confidence radius from Hoeffding’s inequality — a concentration bound on a sample mean. The sum is a linear combination of an estimate and its uncertainty.	UCT adds a mean and a concentration-derived radius.	Exact linear.
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	11	PUCT with a Policy Prior	exact_linear	probabilistic,coupled,graph	A prior-weighted exploration term.	The network’s policy prior multiplies the exploration bonus, so search effort is allocated proportionally to prior probability — a reweighting of the visit distribution. AlphaZero’s strength comes from this coupling of a learned vector with a search rule.	The prior is a multiplicative weight on the exploration term.	Exact linear.
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	12	Constraint Satisfaction Arc Consistency	structural_analogue	operator	A fixed-point computation on domain sets.	Repeatedly pruning domains is a monotone decreasing operator on a lattice, so it converges to a greatest fixed point. Over the Boolean semiring the constraint propagation is a matrix operation on the constraint incidence structure.	Propagation is a monotone fixed point on a lattice.	Structural analogue.
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	13	STRIPS Planning Representation	exact_linear	structural	State vectors over propositions with additive and subtractive updates.	States are binary vectors, and actions add and remove entries — an update s′ = (s∨a+)∧¬a−, which is linear over the Boolean semiring. Planning is reachability in that space, and it is PSPACE-complete.	State updates are Boolean-semiring linear operations.	Exact linear (Boolean semiring).
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	14	Relaxed Plan Heuristic	exact_linear	structural	A relaxation removing the subtractive part of the update.	Dropping deletes makes the state monotonically increasing, so reachability becomes a simple closure computation — the relaxation converts a hard problem into a monotone one. That monotonicity is exactly why the relaxed problem is polynomial.	Dropping deletes makes the update monotone.	Exact linear (Boolean semiring).
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	15	Propositional Entailment	exact_linear	probabilistic,combinatorial	A semantic consequence relation.	Entailment reduces to unsatisfiability, which is NP-complete in general. Over 𝔽2, XOR clauses are solved by Gaussian elimination — the linear fragment is easy, and general SAT’s hardness comes precisely from leaving it.	The linear (XOR) fragment is Gaussian elimination; general SAT is not.	Exact linear (XOR fragment only).
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	16	Resolution Rule	no_faithful_form	combinatorial	An inference rule that is complete for refutation.	Resolution is a combinatorial closure operation on clause sets, and the resolution proof’s length can be exponential. It is a symbolic rule with no additive structure.	Resolution is a symbolic closure with no linear form.	No faithful linear form.
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	17	DPLL Unit Propagation	structural_analogue	structural	A constraint propagation with backtracking.	Unit propagation is a monotone closure on partial assignments — the same lattice fixed point as arc consistency. Backtracking is the non-monotone part and is where the exponential lives.	Propagation is a monotone closure; backtracking is not linear.	Structural analogue.
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	18	Conflict-Driven Clause Learning	exact_linear	dynamical,graph,learning	A cut in an implication graph producing a new constraint.	The learned clause comes from a cut separating the conflict from the decisions — a graph cut, which is the primal side of a max-flow problem. That graph-theoretic step is what turned SAT solvers into industrial tools.	Clause learning is a cut in an implication graph.	Exact linear (the cut).
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	19	First-Order Unification	structural_analogue	structural	A most general substitution — a term-level solve.	Unification is solving equations in a free term algebra, computable in near-linear time. It is a solve, but over a non-linear algebraic structure — substitution does not distribute like a linear map.	Unification solves equations in a free algebra, not a vector space.	Structural analogue.
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	20	Description Logic Subsumption	structural_analogue	structural	A partial order on concepts.	Concepts form a lattice under subsumption with meet and join given by conjunction and disjunction — the same lattice structure as subspaces under inclusion. That analogy is the closest linear correspondence available.	Concepts form a lattice, analogous to subspace inclusion.	Structural analogue.
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	21	Default Logic Extension	no_faithful_form	operator	A non-monotonic inference rule.	Adding information can retract conclusions, so the consequence operator is non-monotone — the opposite of every closure operator above. Non-monotonicity is fundamentally incompatible with a linear (hence monotone in the relevant sense) formulation.	Non-monotonicity has no linear counterpart.	No faithful linear form.
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	22	Bayesian Network Factorisation	exact_linear	probabilistic,graph,geometric	A tensor factorised into low-order conditional tables.	The joint is a huge tensor, and the factorisation expresses it as a product of small ones — exactly a tensor network. The parameter count drops from exponential to the sum of the local table sizes, which is the whole point.	A Bayesian network is a tensor factorisation.	Exact linear (tensor network).
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	23	D-Separation	exact_linear	operator,graph	A graphical criterion for factorisation of the joint.	Conditional independence means the corresponding conditional joint has rank one when viewed as a matrix over the two variable sets. D-separation reads off which such rank-one factorisations hold from the graph alone.	Conditional independence is a rank-one factorisation of a conditional. The LKVexa Compendium — Calculus Edition 23	Exact linear.
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	24	Variable Elimination	exact_linear	graph,geometric	A tensor contraction — summing out an index.	Each elimination step multiplies factors (an outer product) and sums an index (a contraction), so the whole algorithm is a sequence of tensor contractions. The cost is exponential in the largest intermediate factor’s size, which is the induced width of the elimination order — choosing the order is exactly the tensor-network contraction ordering problem. The LKVexa Compendium — Calculus Edition 24	Inference is tensor contraction; cost is the contraction width.	Exact linear.
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	25	Belief Propagation	exact_linear	operator,graph	Message passing — repeated matrix–vector products along edges.	Each message is a matrix–vector product with the pairwise potential matrix, followed by normalisation. On trees it converges exactly in two sweeps; on loopy graphs it may not converge, and its fixed points are stationary points of the Bethe free energy.	Message passing is matrix–vector multiplication along edges.	Exact linear. The LKVexa Compendium — Calculus Edition 25
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	26	Junction Tree Complexity	exact_linear	graph,combinatorial,geometric	A cost exponential in the treewidth.	Treewidth measures how far the graph is from a tree, and it is exactly the largest clique in a chordal completion — the maximal intermediate tensor’s order. It is the single parameter determining whether exact inference is feasible.	Treewidth is the maximum contraction rank.	Exact linear.
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	27	Markov Random Field	exact_linear	operator,probabilistic,dynamical,graph	An undirected factorisation over cliques.	For Gaussian MRFs the precision matrix’s sparsity pattern is the graph — a zero in Σ−1 means conditional independence. That equivalence between graph structure and matrix sparsity is the cleanest statement in graphical models.	For Gaussians, the graph is the precision matrix’s sparsity pattern.	Exact linear (Gaussian case).
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	28	Conditional Random Field	exact_linear	operator,dynamical	A log-linear model over structured outputs.	Exactly linear in the feature functions on the log scale, so the model is a softmax over an exponentially large output space. The forward–backward algorithm computes 𝑍by matrix products over the chain, making inference tractable.	CRFs are log-linear; inference is a matrix product over the chain.	Exact linear in logs.
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	29	Particle Filter Weight Update	exact_linear	operator	A multiplicative reweighting of a discrete measure.	The posterior is represented by a weighted point mass vector, and each update multiplies entrywise by the likelihood and renormalises — a diagonal operator on the weight vector. It is the sampled version of the master equation’s linear update.	The update is a diagonal reweighting of a discrete measure.	Exact linear.
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	30	Effective Sample Size for Particles	exact_linear	structural	The reciprocal squared norm of the weight vector.	Exactly 1/ ‖w‖ 2 2 for normalised weights, equalling 𝑁when uniform and one when degenerate. Weight degeneracy is the weight vector collapsing onto a single coordinate — a rank-one collapse.	ESS is a reciprocal squared norm.	Exact linear.
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	31	Variational Inference Objective	exact_linear	operator,variational,informational,geometric	A lower bound whose gap is a KL divergence.	Maximising the ELBO is minimising 𝐷𝐾𝐿(𝑞‖𝑝) — the projection of the true posterior onto a tractable family in KL geometry. For mean-field families, coordinate ascent updates each factor in closed form, a sequence of linear-in-log updates.	Variational inference is a projection in KL geometry.	Exact linear (mean-field updates).
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	32	Dempster–Shafer Combination	exact_linear	operator	A convolution over the subset lattice.	The combination rule is a Möbius-transform convolution on the lattice of subsets, computable by fast transforms analogous to the FFT. Its normalisation by conflict 𝐾produces counterintuitive results when the sources strongly disagree.	Combination is a lattice convolution with a fast transform.	Exact linear.
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	33	Markov Decision Process	exact_linear	operator,probabilistic,geometric	A transition tensor 𝑃(𝑠′ ∣𝑠, 𝑎) and a reward vector.	For a fixed policy, 𝑃𝜋is a stochastic matrix and the problem becomes a linear system — the whole of policy evaluation. The action dimension is what makes optimisation nonlinear, via the max.	An MDP is a stochastic tensor plus a reward vector.	Exact linear.
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	34	Bellman Expectation Equation	exact_linear	spectral,operator	A linear system in the value vector.	Exactly v = (𝐼−𝛾𝑃𝜋)−1r, and the inverse exists because 𝛾< 1 makes 𝛾𝑃𝜋a contraction with spectral radius below one. Policy evaluation is literally a matrix inversion, and the Neumann series ∑𝛾𝑘(𝑃𝜋)𝑘is the discounted return.	Policy evaluation is a linear solve; the return is a Neumann series.	Exact linear. The field’s central matrix equation.
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	35	Bellman Optimality Equation	linearized	operator,variational	A fixed point of a max-aﬀine operator.	The max makes it nonlinear, but the operator is still a 𝛾-contraction in the sup norm — so a unique fixed point exists by Banach. Each policy’s evaluation is linear; the max over policies is what is not.	The operator is max-aﬀine: linear per policy, nonlinear across them.	Linearised (per policy).
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	36	Q-Function	exact_linear	operator,dynamical	A value matrix indexed by state and action.	𝑄is a matrix where 𝑉is a vector — the extra action index is what makes greedy improvement possible without a model. Its low-rank structure in many problems is what value factorisation methods exploit.	𝑄is the state–action value matrix.	Exact linear.
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	37	Value Iteration Contraction	exact_linear	geometric	A contraction mapping with modulus 𝛾.	Convergence is geometric at rate 𝛾, so the number of iterations for accuracy 𝜀is log 𝜀/ log 𝛾— the same reciprocal-log structure as any linear iterative solver. The discount factor is exactly the convergence rate.	Value iteration is a contraction; 𝛾is its rate.	Exact linear.
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	38	Q-Learning Update	exact_linear	spectral,operator,variational,probabilistic,learning	A stochastic approximation step toward a Bellman target.	Exactly Robbins–Monro stochastic approximation on the optimality operator, converging under the usual step-size conditions. With linear function approximation the update becomes a stochastic linear iteration, and off-policy sampling can make its expected update matrix unstable — the deadly triad is a spectral radius problem.	Q-learning is stochastic approximation; instability is a spectral radius issue.	Exact linear (with linear features).
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	39	SARSA Update	exact_linear	operator,learning	The on-policy counterpart, without the max.	Removing the max makes the expected update exactly linear in 𝑄, which is why SARSA with linear approximation has convergence guarantees that Q-learning lacks. The difference between the two algorithms is precisely the difference between a linear and a max-aﬀine operator.	SARSA’s expected update is linear; Q-learning’s is not.	Exact linear.
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	40	TD(𝜆) Eligibility Traces	exact_linear	variational,geometric	An exponentially weighted accumulation of gradients.	The trace is a geometric filter applied to the gradient sequence — a linear recursion. The forward view shows TD(𝜆) is a geometrically weighted average of 𝑛-step returns, so 𝜆interpolates linearly between TD(0) and Monte Carlo.	Traces are a geometric filter; 𝜆is a linear interpolation weight.	Exact linear.
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	41	Policy Gradient Theorem	exact_linear	variational,probabilistic	An expectation of a score function times a value.	The gradient of the objective involves no derivative of the state distribution — that term cancels exactly, which is the theorem’s content. The remaining expression is a linear functional of 𝑄weighted by the score.	The theorem is an exact cancellation leaving a linear functional.	Exact linear.
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	42	Advantage Function and Baseline	exact_linear	operator,variational	A centring of the value matrix by its row means. The LKVexa Compendium — Calculus Edition 41	Subtracting any state-dependent baseline leaves the gradient unbiased, because the score function has zero mean under the policy — an orthogonality condition. The variance-minimising baseline is a weighted mean, and 𝑉is a good approximation to it.	Baselines exploit an orthogonality; centring reduces variance.	Exact linear.
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	43	Generalised Advantage Estimation	exact_linear	control,geometric	A geometrically weighted sum of TD errors.	Exactly a geometric filter applied to the TD error sequence, so 𝜆trades bias against variance along a one-parameter family. It is the same exponential weighting as eligibility traces, seen from the forward direction.	GAE is a geometric filter on TD errors. The LKVexa Compendium — Calculus Edition 42	Exact linear.
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	44	Trust Region Policy Constraint	exact_linear	operator,variational,informational,geometric	A quadratic constraint given by the Fisher information matrix.	To second order the KL divergence is 1 2Δ𝜃⊤𝐹Δ𝜃, so the trust region is an ellipsoid in the Fisher metric. The natural gradient 𝐹−1∇𝐽is the steepest ascent direction in that metric — a linear solve against the Fisher matrix.	The trust region is a Fisher-metric ellipsoid; the natural gradient is a linear solve.	Exact linear (second order).
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	45	PPO Clipped Objective	exact_linear	operator,variational,probabilistic	A piecewise linear surrogate in the probability ratio.	The clip makes the objective piecewise linear in 𝑟𝑡with zero gradient outside the trust band — a rectifier that removes the incentive to move too far. It approximates the trust region constraint without the Fisher matrix solve.	Clipping is a rectifier producing a piecewise linear objective.	Exact linear (piecewise).
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	46	Soft Actor-Critic Objective	exact_linear	operator,variational,probabilistic,informational,learning	A reward objective plus an entropy bonus.	The optimal policy becomes a Boltzmann distribution over Q-values with temperature 𝛼— a softmax, the same normalised weight vector as everywhere else. Entropy regularisation smooths the max into a log-sum- exp, which is the soft Bellman operator.	Entropy regularisation replaces max with log-sum-exp; the policy is a softmax.	Exact linear apart from the softmax.
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	47	POMDP Belief Update	exact_linear	operator,probabilistic	A matrix–vector product followed by a diagonal reweighting.	Exactly the forward algorithm of an HMM: propagate by the transition matrix, reweight by the observation likelihood, renormalise. The belief is a probability vector and the update is linear before normalisation, so a POMDP is an MDP on the belief simplex.	Belief update is a linear operator plus a normalisation.	Exact linear.
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	48	Value of Information	exact_linear	structural	A difference of two expectations with max and expectation interchanged.	Non-negative by Jensen’s inequality, since the max of an expectation is at most the expectation of a max — information cannot hurt a rational agent. The gap is exactly the Jensen slack, hence a curvature quantity.	VOI is a Jensen gap between max and expectation.	Exact linear.
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	49	Nash Equilibrium in Multi-Agent RL	exact_linear	variational,combinatorial	A fixed point of the best-response correspondence.	For two-player zero-sum games the equilibrium is the value of a linear program, and the minimax theorem is LP duality. For general-sum games it is a complementarity problem — no longer linear, and computing it is PPAD-complete.	Zero-sum equilibria are linear programs; general-sum ones are not.	Exact linear (zero-sum only).
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	50	Fictitious Self-Play	exact_linear	learning	An averaging iteration on the strategy simplex.	The empirical average is a running mean — a linear accumulation — and in zero-sum games the averages converge to equilibrium. Convergence follows from the same argument as no-regret learning.	Self-play averages strategies; convergence is a no-regret result.	Exact linear.
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	51	Counterfactual Regret Minimisation	exact_linear	structural	A rectified regret vector, normalised into a strategy.	Regret matching normalises the positive part of the cumulative regret vector — a rectifier plus an ℓ1 normalisation. Average regret falls as 1/ √ 𝑇, and in zero-sum games that gives convergence to equilibrium.	The strategy is a normalised rectified regret vector.	Exact linear.
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	52	Shapley Value for Credit Assignment	exact_linear	structural	A weighted average of marginal contributions.	Exactly a linear functional of the characteristic function — the unique one satisfying eﬀiciency, sym- metry, linearity, and the null player axiom. Linearity in 𝑣is literally one of the defining axioms, which makes this the most explicitly linear entry in the chapter.	The Shapley value is the unique linear functional satisfying four axioms.	Exact linear.
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	53	Language Model Objective	exact_linear	operator,probabilistic,dynamical,parallel	A sum of log conditional probabilities.	The chain rule of probability makes the joint log-likelihood exactly additive over positions — a sum, which is why autoregressive training parallelises across positions with causal masking. That masking is a triangular matrix.	The objective is additive in logs; causal masking is a triangular matrix.	Exact linear in logs.
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	54	Perplexity	exact_linear	probabilistic,informational,geometric	The exponential of the mean negative log-likelihood.	Exactly the geometric mean of the inverse probabilities, so it is a linear quantity in log space. It is interpretable as an effective branching factor — the size of a uniform distribution with the same entropy.	Perplexity is exponentiated mean surprisal.	Exact linear in logs.
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	55	Temperature Sampling	exact_linear	probabilistic	A scalar rescaling of the logit vector.	Dividing logits by 𝑇is a linear map before the softmax, so temperature interpolates between the argmax (𝑇→0) and the uniform distribution (𝑇→∞). It is a one-parameter family of reweightings.	Temperature is a scalar scaling of the logits.	Exact linear.
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	56	Nucleus (Top-p) Sampling	exact_linear	probabilistic	A truncation of the sorted probability vector.	Sorting is a permutation and truncation is a masking, so the operation is a permutation, a cumulative sum, and a threshold — all linear except the threshold. It adapts the support size to the distribution’s shape, unlike top-k.	Nucleus sampling is a permutation, cumulative sum, and mask.	Exact linear.
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	57	Beam Search	exact_linear	graph	A bounded-width search maximising an additive score.	Scores accumulate additively in log space, so beam search is an approximate max over paths in a lattice — a truncated tropical computation. Length normalisation is a division that corrects the bias toward short sequences.	Beam search is truncated max-plus path search.	Exact linear (tropical, truncated).
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	58	RLHF Reward Model	exact_linear	dynamical,learning	A Bradley–Terry model — logistic in a score difference.	The log odds of a preference are exactly the difference of two scalar scores, so the reward model is identified only up to an additive constant. Fitting it is logistic regression on pairwise contrasts.	Preference learning is logistic regression on score differences.	Exact linear in the logit.
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	59	KL-Regularised Policy Objective	exact_linear	informational	A reward maximisation penalised by divergence from a reference.	The closed-form optimum is 𝜋∗∝𝜋ref exp(𝑟/𝛽) — an exponential tilting of the reference, which is a diagonal reweighting followed by normalisation. That closed form is the basis of the next entry.	The optimum is an exponential tilting of the reference policy.	Exact linear (the tilting).
61	Artificial Intelligence — Calculus Edition	60	Direct Preference Optimisation	exact_linear	dynamical,learning	A logistic loss on a difference of log-ratio terms.	Inverting entry 59’s closed form expresses the implicit reward as a log ratio, so the reward model is eliminated algebraically and preference learning becomes direct supervised training. The whole derivation is a change of variables in log space — one of the cleaner algebraic simplifications in recent machine learning.	DPO substitutes the closed-form optimum, turning RL into logistic regression on log ratios.	Exact linear in logs.
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	1	Homogeneous Transformation	exact_linear	operator	A 4 × 4 matrix combining rotation and translation.	Composition of transforms is matrix multiplication, and inversion is 𝑇−1 = ( 𝑅⊤−𝑅⊤p 0⊤ 1 ) — closed form, no numerical inversion needed. Embedding translation into a linear map on homogeneous coordinates is the trick that makes aﬀine motion linear.	Rigid motion is matrix multiplication in homogeneous coordinates.	Exact linear. Already native.
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	2	Rotation Matrix Properties	exact_linear	operator,geometric	The special orthogonal group 𝑆𝑂(3).	Orthogonality means the columns are an orthonormal frame, so 𝑅−1 = 𝑅⊤and rotations preserve lengths and angles exactly. The constraint surface is a three-dimensional manifold inside nine-dimensional matrix space — six constraints, three degrees of freedom.	Rotations are orthogonal matrices; the group is a constraint manifold.	Exact linear.
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	3	Euler Angles and Gimbal Lock	exact_linear	control	A product of three elementary rotation matrices.	Gimbal lock occurs when the parametrisation’s Jacobian loses rank — two axes align and a degree of freedom is lost. It is a chart singularity, not a physical one, and it exists because no three-parameter chart covers 𝑆𝑂(3) globally.	Gimbal lock is a rank deficiency of the parametrisation Jacobian.	Exact linear, with a chart singularity.
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	4	Axis–Angle and Rodrigues Formula	exact_linear	operator,geometric	The exponential map from a skew-symmetric matrix.	𝑅= 𝑒𝜃[ ̂𝜔]× — the exponential of a skew matrix is orthogonal, which is the Lie algebra to Lie group map. Rodrigues’ formula is the closed form because [ ̂𝜔]3 × = −[ ̂𝜔]× truncates the series.	Rotation is the matrix exponential of a skew matrix.	Exact linear.
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	5	Unit Quaternion Rotation	exact_linear	structural	A conjugation in the quaternion algebra.	Quaternions double-cover 𝑆𝑂(3), so 𝑞and −𝑞give the same rotation — the group 𝑆3 maps two-to-one onto 𝑆𝑂(3). They avoid gimbal lock because 𝑆3 is a global chart, and composition is a bilinear product.	Quaternion multiplication is bilinear; the map to 𝑆𝑂(3) is two-to-one.	Exact linear.
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	6	Twist and Screw Motion	exact_linear	structural	An element of the Lie algebra 𝔰𝔢(3) — a six-vector.	Twists form a vector space, so they can be added and scaled, unlike the group elements themselves. Chasles’ theorem says every rigid motion is a screw — a rotation about an axis with translation along it, which is the twist’s canonical decomposition.	Twists live in a six-dimensional Lie algebra — a genuine vector space.	Exact linear.
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	7	Denavit–Hartenberg Transform	exact_linear	operator,control	A product of four elementary transforms per joint.	The convention reduces six pose parameters to four by aligning frames with the joint axes — a canonical form removing two redundant degrees of freedom. It is a basis choice that minimises parameters.	DH is a canonical parametrisation reducing six parameters to four.	Exact linear.
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	8	Forward Kinematics	exact_linear	operator,control	An ordered product of joint transforms.	Exactly a matrix product, evaluated in 𝑂(𝑛) — the map from joint angles to end-effector pose. It is a smooth but nonlinear map from the joint torus to 𝑆𝐸(3); the linearity is in the composition, not in the dependence on q.	Forward kinematics is a matrix product, nonlinear in the joint angles.	Exact linear (the composition).
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	9	Manipulator Jacobian	exact_linear	spectral,operator,variational,control	A 6 × 𝑛matrix mapping joint rates to end-effector twist.	The central object of manipulator analysis: its column space is the set of achievable twists, its null space is the internal motions that do not move the tool, and its singular values are the gains in each direction. Force maps transpose: 𝜏= 𝐽⊤ℱ, an exact duality.	The Jacobian is a linear map; kinematics and statics are dual through it.	Exact linear.
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	10	Inverse Kinematics via Pseudoinverse	exact_linear	operator,control	A minimum-norm solution plus a null space projection.	𝐽+ gives the least-norm solution; (𝐼−𝐽+𝐽) projects onto ker 𝐽, so redundant arms can pursue secondary objectives without disturbing the tool. This single equation is the reason redundancy is useful, and it is pure pseudoinverse algebra.	IK is a pseudoinverse solve plus a null space projection.	Exact linear.
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	11	Damped Least Squares	exact_linear	spectral,learning,control,exact_numeric	A Tikhonov-regularised inverse.	Adding 𝜆2𝐼shifts every singular value away from zero, so the solution stays bounded near singularities — exactly ridge regression applied to a kinematic solve. The damping trades tracking accuracy for conditioning.	Damping is Tikhonov regularisation of the Jacobian inverse.	Exact linear.
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	12	Manipulability Measure	exact_linear	spectral,control,exact_numeric	The product of the Jacobian’s singular values. The LKVexa Compendium — Calculus Edition 13	The volume of the manipulability ellipsoid, whose axes are the singular vectors scaled by the singular values. It vanishes exactly at singularities, so maximising it is a conditioning objective.	Manipulability is the product of singular values.	Exact linear.
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	13	Kinematic Singularity	exact_linear	spectral,learning,control	A rank deficiency of the Jacobian. The LKVexa Compendium — Calculus Edition 14	At a singularity some direction becomes unreachable and the required joint velocities blow up — the smallest singular value hits zero, so the condition number diverges. Every singularity in robotics is this one linear-algebraic event.	Singularity is Jacobian rank loss.	Exact linear.
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	14	Differential Drive Kinematics	exact_linear	operator,dynamical,control,geometric	A 2 × 2 linear map from wheel speeds to body twist.	Exactly invertible, so wheel commands follow from a desired twist by a matrix inverse. The sum and difference structure means it is a change of basis to symmetric and antisymmetric modes. The LKVexa Compendium — Calculus Edition 15	The map is an invertible 2 × 2 matrix.	Exact linear.
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	15	Ackermann Steering	exact_linear	dynamical,control,geometric	A geometric constraint for slip-free turning.	Both wheels must be tangent to circles about a common centre, which is a linear condition on the cotangents. The bicycle model linearises this to a single steering angle, which is the standard control abstraction.	The constraint is linear in the cotangents.	Exact linear.
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	16	Nonholonomic Constraint	exact_linear	control,parallel	A linear constraint on velocities that is not integrable.	Linear in ̇q but not derivable from a positional constraint, so the reachable configuration space remains full-dimensional even though instantaneous motions are restricted. Chow’s theorem: the Lie brackets of the allowed vector fields span everything — a car can reach any pose by parallel parking, and the bracket is the algebraic reason.	The constraint is linear in velocity; controllability comes from Lie brackets.	Exact linear (the constraint), with a non-integrable structure.
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	17	Manipulator Equation of Motion	exact_linear	operator,dynamical,coupled,geometric	A configuration-dependent mass matrix and a velocity coupling matrix.	𝑀is symmetric positive definite, so it defines a metric on the configuration space — the kinetic energy is a quadratic form in ̇q. Linear in ̈q, which is why forward dynamics is a linear solve at each instant.	The dynamics are linear in acceleration with a positive definite mass matrix.	Exact linear (in acceleration).
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	18	Skew-Symmetry Property	exact_linear	operator,control	A structural identity linking the mass and Coriolis matrices.	Skew-symmetry means ̇q⊤( ̇ 𝑀−2𝐶) ̇q = 0, so the Coriolis terms do no net work — energy conservation, expressible entirely as a matrix property. It is what makes passivity-based control provably stable.	Energy conservation is a skew-symmetry of a matrix combination.	Exact linear.
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	19	Recursive Newton–Euler Algorithm	exact_linear	control	A forward and backward sweep along the kinematic chain.	The chain structure makes the system matrices banded, so the sweeps are exactly forward and backward substitution on a bidiagonal system. Linear cost instead of cubic is the payoff of that structure.	The algorithm is forward–backward substitution on a banded system.	Exact linear.
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	20	Composite Rigid Body Inertia	exact_linear	operator	An assembly of the mass matrix from link inertias.	Each entry is an inner product of motion subspaces with accumulated inertias, so the matrix is built column by column. Its symmetry halves the work.	The mass matrix is assembled from inner products.	Exact linear.
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	21	Articulated Body Algorithm	exact_linear	probabilistic	A propagation of articulated inertias down the chain.	Avoids forming and inverting 𝑀by propagating Schur complements along the chain — each joint’s elimination is a rank-one update to the downstream inertia. It is exactly banded Gaussian elimination in physical clothing.	The algorithm is banded elimination via Schur complements.	Exact linear.
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	22	Operational Space Formulation	exact_linear	operator,control	The task-space inertia — a congruence transform of the joint-space one.	Λ is the inverse of the projected inverse inertia, which is exactly a Schur complement structure. It lets control be specified in task coordinates while null-space motions are handled separately by the dynamically consistent pseudoinverse.	Task-space inertia is a congruence transform and Schur complement.	Exact linear.
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	23	Contact Wrench Cone	exact_linear	variational	A second-order cone constraint on contact forces.	The friction cone is convex, so feasibility problems over it are second-order cone programs. Polyhedral approximation replaces it with a set of linear inequalities, which is why most solvers use a linearised friction pyramid.	The cone is convex; linearising gives a polyhedron.	Exact linear (linearised).
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	24	Zero Moment Point	exact_linear	variational	A weighted average of link positions — a generalised centre of pressure.	Exactly a normalised inner product of positions with force weights, so the ZMP is a point in the convex hull of contact points when balanced. Stability is the linear condition that it lies inside the support polygon.	The ZMP is a weighted average; stability is a convex containment.	Exact linear.
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	25	Linear Inverted Pendulum Model	exact_linear	spectral,dynamical,control	A linear second-order system with eigenvalues ±√𝑔/𝑧𝑐.	Constraining the centre of mass to a horizontal plane makes the dynamics exactly linear, which is the whole reason the model dominates walking control. One eigenvalue is unstable and one stable — the unstable one is what capture point control cancels.	The constrained pendulum is an exactly linear system.	Exact linear.
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	26	Capture Point	exact_linear	spectral,operator,dynamical,control	A projection onto the unstable eigenvector.	The capture point is exactly the coordinate along the unstable eigendirection — stepping to it cancels the divergent mode and brings the robot to rest. It is one of the cleanest applications of eigenvector decomposition in engineering practice.	The capture point is the unstable modal coordinate.	Exact linear.
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	27	Configuration Space	structural_analogue	geometric	A manifold, not a vector space.	For a revolute arm 𝒞= 𝑇𝑛, a torus — which is locally like ℝ𝑛but globally is not, so straight-line interpolation is not well defined without care. That topological difference is why planning is not simply a linear problem.	𝒞is a manifold; only local charts are linear.	Structural analogue.
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	28	Minkowski Sum Obstacle Growth	exact_linear	variational	A set-valued sum.	For convex sets the Minkowski sum’s support function is the sum of the support functions — an exactly linear operation on the support function representation. That linearity is what makes convex collision checking eﬀicient.	Minkowski sums add support functions linearly.	Exact linear (in support function space).
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	29	Probabilistic Roadmap Coverage	exact_linear	probabilistic,graph	An exponential coverage guarantee.	Each sample independently covers a region, so the failure probability decays exponentially in sample count — a product of independent complements. The roadmap itself is a graph, and querying it is a shortest path computation.	Coverage is exponential in samples; queries are graph searches.	Exact linear in logs.
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	30	RRT Growth	structural_analogue	geometric	A tree growing by nearest-neighbour extension.	A uniformly sampled point is most likely to fall in the largest Voronoi cell, so growth is automatically biased toward frontiers. The Voronoi diagram is a partition induced by a distance metric — the geometry does the exploration.	Growth bias follows from Voronoi cell volumes under a metric.	Structural analogue.
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	31	RRT* Rewiring Radius	exact_linear	variational,graph,geometric	A shrinking connection radius ensuring asymptotic optimality.	The radius must shrink slowly enough to keep the random geometric graph connected — the same log 𝑛/𝑛threshold as in percolation on random graphs. The 1/𝑑exponent is where dimensionality bites.	The radius is a random geometric graph connectivity threshold.	Exact linear in logs.
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	32	Artificial Potential Field	exact_linear	variational,dynamical,control	A sum of gradient fields.	Forces superpose exactly because gradients add, so composing behaviours is linear. Local minima are where the gradients cancel — the method’s well-known failure mode, and it is a statement about the potential’s critical points.	Potentials add; failures are critical points of the sum.	Exact linear.
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	33	Dynamic Window Approach	exact_linear	structural	A weighted sum of objectives over an admissible velocity set.	Exactly a linear scalarisation of a multi-objective problem, evaluated over a box of dynamically feasible velocities. Weight choice determines the trade-off — a point on the Pareto frontier.	The objective is a linear scalarisation over a feasible box.	Exact linear.
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	34	Velocity Obstacle	exact_linear	structural	A cone in velocity space.	The set of colliding relative velocities is a cone, and avoiding it is a constraint set complement. Reciprocal velocity obstacles split the avoidance effort by a linear half-and-half rule, which makes the cone constraint a half-plane.	Collision avoidance is a cone constraint; the reciprocal version is a half-plane. The LKVexa Compendium — Calculus Edition 34	Exact linear.
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	35	Trajectory Optimisation Objective	linearized	variational,dynamical,control	A quadratic control cost plus a nonconvex obstacle term.	Without obstacles it is a linear-quadratic problem with a closed-form solution; obstacles make it nonconvex, so solvers sequentially convexify — solving a sequence of quadratic programs. That sequence is the workhorse of modern trajectory optimisation.	The problem is solved as a sequence of quadratic programs.	Linearised (sequentially).
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	36	Minimum Jerk Trajectory	exact_linear	operator,variational	A quintic polynomial fitted to six boundary conditions.	Six conditions and six coeﬀicients give a square linear system with a Vandermonde matrix, solved exactly. The minimum-jerk objective’s Euler–Lagrange equation is 𝑥(6) = 0, whose solutions are exactly quintics.	The trajectory solves a Vandermonde linear system.	Exact linear.
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	37	Dubins Path	structural_analogue	variational,graph	A finite set of candidate path types.	The optimal path is always one of six combinations of arcs and straight segments, so optimisation reduces to enumerating six closed-form candidates and taking the minimum. It is a discrete selection over analytically solved pieces, not a linear optimisation.	Optimisation reduces to a finite enumeration.	Structural analogue.
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	38	Kalman Filter Prediction	exact_linear	operator,probabilistic,control	A linear state propagation and a congruence transform of the covariance.	The state map is linear and the covariance transforms by congruence — exactly how a quadratic form transforms under a change of variables. Adding 𝑄is the process noise injection.	Prediction is a linear map plus a congruence transform.	Exact linear.
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	39	Kalman Filter Update	exact_linear	variational,probabilistic,control	The optimal linear gain — a weighted least squares solution.	The gain weights prediction against measurement by their relative precisions, and the result is simul- taneously the BLUE, the MAP estimate under Gaussian assumptions, and the recursive least squares solution. The updated covariance (𝐼−𝐾𝐻)𝑃−is a Schur complement.	The update is a weighted least squares solve; the covariance is a Schur complement.	Exact linear.
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	40	Extended Kalman Filter Linearisation	linearized	variational,informational,control	A Jacobian evaluated at the current estimate.	The EKF applies the exactly-linear Kalman machinery to a first-order approximation, so its optimality guarantees are lost. Divergence occurs when the linearisation point drifts far from the truth — the error of the approximation feeds back into the estimate.	The EKF linearises then applies the linear filter.	Linearised.
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	41	Unscented Transform	exact_linear	operator,probabilistic,control	A deterministic quadrature capturing two moments.	The sigma points are chosen so their weighted mean and covariance match exactly, and propagating them captures the transformed distribution to higher order than a Jacobian does. The point placement uses a matrix square root of the covariance — a Cholesky factor.	Sigma points come from a covariance matrix square root.	Exact linear (the point construction).
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	42	Mahalanobis Gating	exact_linear	probabilistic,parallel,geometric	A quadratic form in the innovation with the innovation covariance as metric.	Exactly a whitened distance, chi-square distributed under correct association. Data association is thresholding this quadratic form, which is why the innovation covariance must be right.	Gating is a whitened quadratic form.	Exact linear.
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	43	Monte Carlo Localisation	exact_linear	probabilistic,control	A weighted particle approximation to a pose distribution.	Motion propagates particles through the (nonlinear) kinematics and observations reweight them di- agonally — the same linear-then-normalise structure as any particle filter. It handles multimodality, which the Kalman filter’s single Gaussian cannot.	Reweighting is diagonal; the representation handles multimodality.	Exact linear (the reweighting).
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	44	SLAM Posterior	exact_linear	operator,probabilistic	A joint distribution over trajectory and map.	The correlations between landmarks are what make SLAM hard and also what make it work — the covariance matrix is dense even though each observation touches few variables. Its inverse, the information matrix, is sparse, which is the key exploited by entry 46.	The covariance is dense; the information matrix is sparse.	Exact linear.
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	45	Graph SLAM Objective	linearized	graph	A weighted nonlinear least squares problem.	Solved by Gauss–Newton, each iteration is a linear system 𝐻Δx = −b with 𝐻= ∑𝐽⊤Ω𝐽— a sum of rank-limited contributions. Each edge adds a small block, so 𝐻has exactly the graph’s sparsity pattern.	Each iteration is a sparse linear solve with graph-structured 𝐻.	Linearised (Gauss–Newton).
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	46	Information Matrix Sparsity	exact_linear	operator,probabilistic,dynamical	The inverse covariance, whose zeros encode conditional independence.	A zero entry means the two variables are conditionally independent given the rest, and in SLAM each measurement couples only a few variables — so Ω is sparse where Σ is dense. Exploiting this sparsity with sparse Cholesky is what made large-scale SLAM tractable.	Sparsity of the information matrix is conditional independence.	Exact linear.
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	47	Bundle Adjustment	linearized	variational	A large sparse nonlinear least squares problem.	The Hessian has a bipartite block structure (cameras and points), and the Schur complement trick eliminates the points block — which is block-diagonal and trivially invertible — leaving a much smaller camera system. That Schur complement is what makes bundle adjustment feasible on millions of points.	The Schur complement exploits the bipartite Hessian structure.	Linearised (Gauss–Newton with Schur complement).
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	48	Pinhole Camera Projection	exact_linear	operator	A 3 × 4 projection matrix acting on homogeneous coordinates.	Projection is exactly a linear map in homogeneous coordinates followed by a division — so it is linear projectively but not aﬀinely. That projective linearity is what makes the entire structure-from-motion machinery algebraic.	Projection is a linear map in projective coordinates.	Exact linear (projectively).
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	49	Epipolar Constraint	exact_linear	spectral,operator	A bilinear form with a rank-two matrix.	The fundamental matrix has rank exactly two (det 𝐹= 0), and its null vectors are the epipoles. Estimating it from correspondences is a linear system solved by SVD, then projected back to rank two by zeroing the smallest singular value — a textbook Eckart–Young truncation.	𝐹is a rank-two matrix estimated by SVD and rank-truncated.	Exact linear.
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	50	RANSAC Iteration Count	exact_linear	probabilistic,geometric	A required sample count from a geometric distribution.	Each trial independently succeeds with probability 𝑤𝑠, so the count follows from a geometric tail — linear in logs. Sensitivity to the minimal sample size 𝑠is exponential, which is why minimal solvers matter.	The count inverts a geometric probability.	Exact linear in logs.
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	51	Computed Torque Control	exact_linear	dynamical,control	An exact feedback linearisation of the manipulator dynamics.	Cancelling the nonlinear terms makes the closed-loop error dynamics exactly ̈e + 𝐾𝐷̇e + 𝐾𝑃e = 0 — a linear second-order system with placeable poles. It converts a nonlinear plant into a linear one by exact cancellation, and its weakness is that the cancellation requires a perfect model.	Feedback linearisation makes the error dynamics exactly linear.	Exact linear (after cancellation).
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	52	PD Plus Gravity Compensation	exact_linear	dynamical	A linear feedback law with one nonlinear correction.	Global asymptotic stability follows from a Lyapunov argument using the skew-symmetry property of entry 18 — no exact model of 𝑀or 𝐶is needed. That robustness is why it is preferred when the model is uncertain.	Stability follows from a quadratic Lyapunov function and skew-symmetry.	Exact linear.
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	53	Impedance Control	exact_linear	spectral,operator,control	A prescribed second-order linear relation between motion and force.	The controller imposes a desired mass–damper–spring matrix triple, so the robot behaves as a chosen linear mechanical system. Choosing 𝐾𝑑and 𝐵𝑑is choosing the closed-loop eigenvalues.	Impedance control assigns a second-order linear system.	Exact linear.
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	54	Hybrid Force–Position Control	exact_linear	operator,control	A selection matrix splitting the task space.	𝑆is a projection: idempotent, splitting directions into force-controlled and position-controlled sub- spaces. The two subspaces must be orthogonal complements, which is exactly the constraint that a projection imposes.	The selection matrix is a projection onto complementary subspaces.	Exact linear.
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	55	Admittance Control	exact_linear	operator,control	A causal inverse of impedance.	Admittance maps force to motion where impedance maps motion to force, so the two are inverse transfer functions. Which one is stable depends on the environment’s stiffness — a coupled stability condition on the product of the two operators.	Admittance is the inverse operator of impedance.	Exact linear.
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	56	Grasp Matrix and Force Closure	exact_linear	operator	A matrix mapping contact forces to an object wrench.	Force closure requires 𝐺to be surjective onto the wrench space and the required contact forces to lie in the friction cone — a rank condition plus a positivity condition. It is exactly the same structure as feasibility in a conic program.	Force closure is a rank condition plus cone membership.	Exact linear.
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	57	Grasp Quality (Ferrari–Canny)	exact_linear	variational,geometric	The radius of the largest ball inscribed in the grasp wrench space.	The grasp wrench space is the convex hull of the contact wrench primitives, and 𝑄is the distance from the origin to its nearest facet. Computing it is a convex hull plus a distance computation.	Grasp quality is a distance from the origin to a convex hull’s boundary.	Exact linear.
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	58	Visual Servoing Control Law	exact_linear	operator,control,geometric	A pseudoinverse of the image Jacobian applied to a feature error.	The interaction matrix 𝐿𝑠maps camera velocity to feature velocity, and the control is a damped pseudoinverse solve — structurally identical to inverse kinematics. Image-based servoing can drive the camera through configurations that are geometrically absurd, because the pseudoinverse minimises image error, not Cartesian error.	Visual servoing is a pseudoinverse solve on the image Jacobian.	Exact linear.
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	59	Model Predictive Control for Robots	exact_linear	variational,dynamical,control	A quadratic program over a finite horizon.	With linear dynamics and quadratic cost the problem is exactly a QP whose Hessian is block-banded — solvable in time linear in the horizon by Riccati recursion. Constraints add inequalities, keeping it a QP. The LKVexa Compendium — Calculus Edition 58	MPC is a banded quadratic program.	Exact linear (quadratic program).
62	Robotics — Calculus Edition	60	Control Barrier Function Safety Constraint	exact_linear	operator,control,parallel	A linear inequality in the control input.	Because ̇ℎ= 𝐿𝑓ℎ+ 𝐿𝑔ℎu is aﬀine in u, the safety condition is a linear constraint — so safe control is a quadratic program that minimally modifies a nominal input. Safety filtering reduces to projecting onto a half-space, which is why it runs at kilohertz rates. The LKVexa Compendium — Calculus Edition 59	Safety is a linear constraint; filtering is a projection.	Exact linear.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	1	Adjacency Matrix	exact_linear	spectral,operator,graph,geometric	The matrix that is the network.	Symmetric for undirected graphs, so its eigenvalues are real and its eigenvectors orthogonal. (𝐴𝑘)𝑖𝑗 counts walks of length 𝑘from 𝑖to 𝑗— the single most useful fact in the field, and it makes every path-based quantity a matrix power.	Already native — the network is a matrix; walks are its powers.	Exact linear.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	2	Degree and Degree Sequence	exact_linear	operator,probabilistic,graph	The vector of row sums, k = 𝐴1.	Exactly the adjacency matrix applied to the all-ones vector, and the degree matrix 𝐷= diag(k) appears throughout. The sequence sorted descending is the degree distribution’s empirical form.	Degree is 𝐴1.	Exact linear.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	3	Handshaking Lemma	exact_linear	operator	A trace-like identity.	Exactly 1⊤𝐴1 = 2𝑚— summing all entries counts each edge twice. It is the simplest conservation statement about the matrix.	The lemma sums all matrix entries.	Exact linear.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	4	Density	exact_linear	operator,coupled,graph	The normalised total of the adjacency matrix.	The mean off-diagonal entry, so density is the matrix’s average. Real networks are sparse, meaning 𝑚= 𝑂(𝑛) rather than 𝑂(𝑛2) — which is why sparse matrix algorithms dominate the computational side.	Density is the mean matrix entry.	Exact linear.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	5	Average Degree and Sparsity	exact_linear	operator,graph	The mean of the degree vector.	Constant average degree as 𝑛grows means the matrix has 𝑂(𝑛) nonzeros, so storage and matrix–vector products cost 𝑂(𝑛). That sparsity is what makes large-network computation feasible at all.	Sparsity bounds the matrix’s nonzero count.	Exact linear.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	6	Degree Distribution	exact_linear	operator,probabilistic,informational,dynamical,graph	The empirical distribution of the row sums.	A first-order summary of the matrix that discards all correlation structure — two networks with identical degree distributions can be entirely different. The configuration model of entry 29 is the maximum entropy network with a given degree sequence.	The distribution summarises the row sums, discarding correlations.	Exact linear.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	7	Power-Law Degree Distribution	exact_linear	spectral,probabilistic,learning	A heavy-tailed degree distribution.	Linear in log–log, and 𝛾< 3 makes the second moment diverge — which is what drives the vanishing epidemic threshold of entry 49 and the spectral radius’ unbounded growth. The exponent is estimated by maximum likelihood, not by naive regression on a histogram.	The distribution is log-linear; 𝛾< 3 makes ⟨𝑘2⟩diverge.	Exact linear in logs.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	8	Excess Degree Distribution	exact_linear	probabilistic,dynamical	The degree distribution seen from the end of an edge.	Size-biased by degree, which is the friendship paradox — your friends have more friends than you, on average, exactly because edges sample nodes proportionally to degree. Its mean is ⟨𝑘2⟩/ ⟨𝑘⟩−1, the branching ratio.	Edge sampling is degree-biased; the mean excess degree is the branching ratio.	Exact linear.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	9	Clustering Coeﬀicient (Local)	exact_linear	operator,dynamical,graph	A normalised count of triangles at a node.	𝑒𝑖is computable from 𝐴2 restricted to 𝑖’s neighbourhood, so the local clustering is a ratio of matrix- power quantities. Averaging over nodes gives a different quantity from entry 10.	Local clustering counts triangles from the adjacency square.	Exact linear.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	10	Transitivity (Global Clustering)	exact_linear	operator,dynamical,coupled	A ratio of two matrix-power functionals.	Literally a trace over an off-diagonal sum — one of the cleanest matrix expressions in the field. It differs from the average local clustering because it weights high-degree nodes more.	Transitivity is tr(𝐴3) over an 𝐴2 sum.	Exact linear.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	11	Shortest Path Length and Diameter	exact_linear	operator,dynamical,graph,geometric	Entries of the min-plus matrix closure.	The distance matrix is 𝐴∗in the min-plus semiring, computable by Floyd–Warshall in 𝑂(𝑛3) or by BFS from each node in 𝑂(𝑛𝑚). Diameter is that matrix’s maximum entry.	Distances are a min-plus matrix closure.	Exact linear (min-plus semiring).
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	12	Degree Assortativity	exact_linear	operator,graph	A normalised quadratic form in the modularity matrix.	The numerator contracts the modularity matrix 𝐵= 𝐴−kk⊤/2𝑚with the degree vector, so assorta- tivity is exactly a Pearson correlation of degrees across edges. Social networks are assortative, technological ones disassortative.	Assortativity is a quadratic form in the modularity matrix.	Exact linear.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	13	Reciprocity	exact_linear	operator,coupled,graph,geometric	A ratio measuring the matrix’s symmetry.	The numerator is tr(𝐴2) for a directed graph, so reciprocity measures how close 𝐴is to symmetric. It is the fraction of the matrix’s mass in its symmetric part.	Reciprocity measures the matrix’s symmetric component.	Exact linear.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	14	Motif Significance	exact_linear	graph	A standardised subgraph count. The LKVexa Compendium — Calculus Edition 15	Small subgraph counts are traces of adjacency polynomials — triangles are tr(𝐴3)/6, and larger motifs need inclusion–exclusion over walk counts. The null preserves the degree sequence, which is a margin-preserving randomisation.	Motif counts are adjacency polynomial traces.	Exact linear.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	15	Degree Centrality	exact_linear	graph	A normalised row sum. The LKVexa Compendium — Calculus Edition 16	The zeroth-order centrality — it counts immediate neighbours and nothing beyond. All the following measures extend it to longer walks.	Degree centrality is a normalised row sum.	Exact linear.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	16	Closeness Centrality	exact_linear	operator,graph,geometric	A reciprocal row sum of the distance matrix.	Undefined for disconnected graphs because some distances are infinite, which is why entry 17 exists. It is a min-plus computation feeding a reciprocal.	Closeness is a reciprocal row sum of the min-plus distance matrix. The LKVexa Compendium — Calculus Edition 17	Exact linear.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	17	Harmonic Centrality	exact_linear	operator,graph,geometric	A sum of reciprocal distances.	Swapping the reciprocal inside the sum makes infinite distances contribute zero, so it handles discon- nected graphs gracefully. It is the same row of the distance matrix, transformed elementwise before summing.	Harmonic centrality transforms before summing rather than after.	Exact linear.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	18	Betweenness Centrality	exact_linear	spectral,graph,learning,combinatorial	A count over shortest path enumerations.	Brandes’ algorithm computes it in 𝑂(𝑛𝑚) by accumulating dependencies along BFS trees — a back- ward accumulation on the shortest path DAG, structurally like backpropagation. It is a path-counting quantity, not a spectral one.	Betweenness is a backward accumulation on shortest path DAGs.	Exact linear.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	19	Eigenvector Centrality	exact_linear	spectral,graph	The Perron eigenvector.	Computable by power iteration x ←𝐴x/ ‖𝐴x‖, which converges at rate |𝜆2/𝜆1|. Perron–Frobenius guarantees a unique positive eigenvector for a connected network.	Eigenvector centrality is the Perron eigenvector via power iteration.	Exact linear.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	20	Katz Centrality	exact_linear	spectral,graph,geometric	A Neumann series over all walk lengths, geometrically discounted.	Exactly ∑𝑘𝛼𝑘𝐴𝑘1, converging when 𝛼< 1/𝜆1 — so Katz centrality is a resolvent evaluation. It fixes eigenvector centrality’s failure on directed acyclic graphs by adding the constant term.	Katz centrality is a resolvent — a discounted sum of walk counts.	Exact linear.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	21	PageRank	exact_linear	operator,probabilistic,graph	The stationary distribution of a damped random walk.	The damping makes the matrix irreducible and aperiodic, guaranteeing a unique stationary vector by Perron–Frobenius — that is the whole reason for 𝛼= 0.85. Convergence rate is exactly 𝛼, so about fifty iterations suﬀice regardless of network size.	PageRank is a stationary distribution; damping guarantees uniqueness and sets the rate.	Exact linear.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	22	HITS Hubs and Authorities	exact_linear	operator,graph	The leading singular vectors of the adjacency matrix.	Substituting gives h = 𝐴𝐴⊤h and a = 𝐴⊤𝐴a, so hubs and authorities are the left and right singular vectors — HITS is the SVD’s leading pair. That identification is exact.	HITS computes the adjacency matrix’s leading singular vectors.	Exact linear.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	23	k-Core Decomposition	structural_analogue	spectral,graph,combinatorial	A nested sequence of subgraphs.	A peeling algorithm running in 𝑂(𝑚), producing a monotone nested family — the cores are ordered by inclusion. It is combinatorial rather than spectral, and it identifies the densely connected nucleus.	Core decomposition is a monotone peeling; it is combinatorial.	Structural analogue.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	24	Subgraph Centrality	exact_linear	spectral,operator,graph,control	A diagonal entry of the adjacency matrix exponential.	Literally ∑𝑘(𝐴𝑘)𝑖𝑖/𝑘! — closed walks of every length, factorially discounted. The matrix exponential’s spectral decomposition makes it computable from 𝐴’s eigenpairs, and the estimation is a Lanczos quadrature.	Subgraph centrality is a diagonal of 𝑒𝐴.	Exact linear.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	25	Percolation and Bridging Centrality	exact_linear	spectral,dynamical,graph	A measure of a node’s role in the component structure.	Removing a node changes the number of connected components, which is the multiplicity of the Laplacian’s zero eigenvalue — so bridging importance is a spectral multiplicity change. Articulation points are the extreme case.	Connectivity role is a Laplacian null space dimension change.	Exact linear.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	26	Erdős–Rényi Random Graph	exact_linear	spectral,operator,probabilistic,graph,geometric	A random symmetric matrix with independent Bernoulli entries.	Its spectrum follows the semicircle law in the dense regime, with the Perron eigenvalue ≈𝑛𝑝separated from the bulk. The degree distribution is binomial, approaching Poisson — much lighter-tailed than real networks.	ER graphs are random matrices with semicircle bulk spectra.	Exact linear.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	27	Giant Component Threshold	exact_linear	spectral,dynamical	A branching process criticality condition.	The expected number of new nodes reached per step is ⟨𝑘⟩, so a giant component appears exactly when that branching ratio crosses one. It is the same unit-spectral-radius condition as every threshold in this chapter.	The threshold is a unit branching ratio.	Exact linear.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	28	Molloy–Reed Criterion	exact_linear	informational,graph	A threshold in terms of the first two degree moments.	The mean excess degree must exceed one, which requires the second moment — so heavy-tailed networks with divergent ⟨𝑘2⟩always have a giant component. That divergence is the source of scale-free networks’ robustness.	The criterion is a second-moment branching condition.	Exact linear.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	29	Configuration Model	exact_linear	operator,informational,dynamical,graph	The maximum entropy ensemble with a given degree sequence.	The expected adjacency is 𝑘𝑖𝑘𝑗/2𝑚— exactly a rank-one matrix, which is why the modularity matrix subtracts it. That rank-one null expectation appears throughout the chapter.	The null expected adjacency is a rank-one outer product.	Exact linear.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	30	Watts–Strogatz Small World	exact_linear	spectral,operator,dynamical,coupled,graph	An interpolation between a circulant and a random matrix.	The lattice’s adjacency is circulant (diagonalised by the Fourier basis) and rewiring adds random off-diagonal entries — so the model interpolates between two spectral extremes. A few rewirings collapse the path length while clustering survives, which is the small-world effect.	The model interpolates between a circulant and a random matrix.	Exact linear.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	31	Barabási–Albert Preferential Attachment	exact_linear	probabilistic	A growth rule producing 𝛾= 3.	The degree distribution’s master equation is linear and solvable exactly, giving the cubic power law. Growth plus preferential attachment are both necessary — either alone gives an exponential distribution.	The degree distribution solves a linear master equation.	Exact linear.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	32	Price Model and Fitness	exact_linear	probabilistic,dynamical	Attachment weighted by an intrinsic fitness.	Fitness lets latecomers overtake, and the resulting distribution is a mixture of power laws — a Bose– Einstein condensation occurs when one fitness dominates. That condensation is a rank-one collapse of the growth process.	Fitness gives a mixture of power laws; condensation is a rank-one collapse.	Exact linear.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	33	Exponential Random Graph Model	exact_linear	variational,informational,dynamical,graph	A maximum entropy family over graphs.	Log-linear in the suﬀicient statistics, so it is the exponential family matched to those statistics. De- generacy arises when the statistics are non-convex functions on the graph space, concentrating the mass at extremes.	ERGM is a log-linear exponential family.	Exact linear in logs.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	34	Stochastic Block Model	exact_linear	spectral,operator,probabilistic,dynamical,graph,control	A low-rank expected adjacency 𝑍𝐵𝑍⊤.	Exactly a rank-𝑘factorisation with a membership indicator matrix, so recovery is a low-rank matrix estimation problem. Spectral methods work because 𝐴’s leading eigenvectors concentrate around its expecta- tion’s.	The SBM is a low-rank factorisation; recovery is spectral.	Exact linear.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	35	Spatial and Geometric Random Graphs	exact_linear	operator,graph,geometric	An adjacency defined by a distance threshold.	High clustering arises automatically because the connection relation is nearly transitive in a metric space, but path lengths grow polynomially rather than logarithmically. Geometry buys clustering and costs small-worldness.	The adjacency is a thresholded distance matrix.	Exact linear.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	36	Hyperbolic Network Model	exact_linear	probabilistic,dynamical,graph,geometric	A geometric graph in negatively curved space.	Exponential volume growth in hyperbolic space produces power-law degrees and high clustering si- multaneously — a geometric explanation for both. Greedy routing succeeds with high probability because the geometry encodes the shortest paths.	Hyperbolic geometry generates scale-free structure from a distance threshold.	Exact linear.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	37	Modularity	exact_linear	spectral,operator	A quadratic form in the modularity matrix 𝐵= 𝐴−kk⊤/2𝑚.	Exactly 1 2𝑚tr(𝑆⊤𝐵𝑆) for an assignment matrix 𝑆, so maximising modularity is a discrete quadratic optimisation. Relaxing 𝑆to be real gives 𝐵’s leading eigenvector — Newman’s spectral bisection, which is exact for the relaxation.	Modularity is a quadratic form in 𝐵; its relaxation is an eigenvector.	Exact linear (relaxed).
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	38	Resolution-Limited Modularity	exact_linear	dynamical,graph	A scale bound intrinsic to the null model.	The null term 𝑘𝑖𝑘𝑗/2𝑚shrinks with network size, so small dense groups become invisible against it — a normalisation artefact rather than a data limitation. The resolution parameter 𝛾rescales that term to tune the detected scale.	The limit is a normalisation of the null term.	Exact linear.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	39	Louvain and Leiden Algorithms	exact_linear	operator,graph	A multilevel greedy optimisation of the modularity form.	Each aggregation step contracts the adjacency matrix into a smaller one — a coarsening, exactly as in algebraic multigrid. Leiden adds a refinement guaranteeing connected communities, which Louvain can violate.	The algorithms are multilevel matrix coarsenings.	Exact linear.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	40	Map Equation (Infomap)	exact_linear	spectral,probabilistic,dynamical	An expected code length for describing a random walk. The LKVexa Compendium — Calculus Edition 40	Minimises the description length of a random walk’s trajectory, so communities are regions where the walk lingers — a compression rather than a density criterion. It uses the walk’s stationary distribution, which is a Perron eigenvector.	Infomap minimises a code length over a random walk’s stationary distribution.	Exact linear.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	41	Normalised Mutual Information	exact_linear	operator,informational	A normalised comparison of two partitions.	Mutual information measures the confusion matrix’s departure from its rank-one margin product, normalised to [0, 1]. It is permutation-invariant because permutations act on both margins.	NMI is a normalised rank-one departure of the confusion matrix.	Exact linear. The LKVexa Compendium — Calculus Edition 41
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	42	Detectability Threshold	exact_linear	spectral,operator,graph	A phase transition below which no algorithm can find the communities.	Exactly the point at which the signal eigenvalue emerges from the random matrix bulk — a BBP tran- sition from random matrix theory. Below it the community structure is present but spectrally indistinguishable from noise, and the impossibility is information-theoretic, not algorithmic.	Detectability is a BBP eigenvalue transition.	Exact linear. One of the field’s sharpest spectral results. The LKVexa Compendium — Calculus Edition 42
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	43	Overlapping Community (Clique Percolation)	exact_linear	operator,graph,combinatorial	A percolation on the clique adjacency structure.	Builds a clique–clique overlap matrix and finds its components, so it is connectivity analysis on a derived matrix. Clique finding is NP-hard in general, which limits it to small 𝑘.	The method finds components of a clique overlap matrix.	Exact linear.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	44	Core–Periphery Structure	exact_linear	operator,graph	A rank-one pattern in the adjacency matrix.	The idealised core–periphery adjacency is cc⊤for a coreness vector — exactly rank one, so detection is a rank-one fit. It is the complement of assortative community structure, which is block diagonal.	Core–periphery is a rank-one adjacency pattern.	Exact linear.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	45	Graph Laplacian	exact_linear	spectral,operator,graph,geometric	A symmetric positive semidefinite matrix.	x⊤𝐿x = ∑(𝑖,𝑗)∈𝐸(𝑥𝑖−𝑥𝑗)2 ≥0, so 𝐿is positive semidefinite with 1 in its null space. The multiplicity of eigenvalue zero equals the number of connected components — an exact rank statement.	The Laplacian is positive semidefinite; its null space counts components.	Exact linear.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	46	Algebraic Connectivity	exact_linear	spectral,graph	The Fiedler value — the second smallest Laplacian eigenvalue.	Zero exactly when the graph is disconnected, and larger values mean better connectivity. It bounds the mixing rate, the synchronisability, and the cut quality — a single number governing many dynamical properties.	Algebraic connectivity is the second Laplacian eigenvalue.	Exact linear.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	47	Cheeger Inequality	exact_linear	spectral,combinatorial	A two-sided bound linking a spectral and a combinatorial quantity.	The conductance ℎ(𝐺) is a combinatorial minimum over cuts — NP-hard to compute — while 𝜆2 is a polynomial-time eigenvalue. The inequality says the spectral relaxation is a provably good approximation, which is the theoretical justification for spectral clustering.	Cheeger bounds a combinatorial cut by a spectral quantity.	Exact linear.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	48	Spectral Clustering	exact_linear	spectral	An eigenvector-based partition.	The Fiedler vector minimises x⊤𝐿x subject to orthogonality with 1 — a Rayleigh quotient minimisa- tion, which is the continuous relaxation of the minimum cut. Using 𝑘eigenvectors and 𝑘-means generalises it to 𝑘clusters.	Spectral clustering minimises a Rayleigh quotient — the relaxed cut.	Exact linear.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	49	Spectral Radius and Epidemic Threshold	exact_linear	spectral,operator,dynamical,graph	The reciprocal of the adjacency matrix’s largest eigenvalue.	Exactly the condition for the linearised epidemic dynamics to have a positive growth eigenvalue, so the epidemic threshold is a spectral radius statement. For scale-free networks with 𝛾< 3, 𝜆1 grows without bound and the threshold vanishes — which is why such networks cannot be protected by uniform vaccination.	The threshold is a reciprocal spectral radius.	Exact linear.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	50	Random Walk Stationary Distribution	exact_linear	spectral,operator,probabilistic,graph	The Perron eigenvector of the transition matrix 𝑃= 𝐷−1𝐴.	Degree-proportional for an undirected connected graph, which is why edge sampling is degree-biased. It is exactly the left eigenvector of 𝑃with eigenvalue one.	The stationary distribution is a Perron eigenvector.	Exact linear.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	51	Mixing Time and Spectral Gap	exact_linear	spectral,operator,graph	A reciprocal spectral gap.	The second eigenvalue of the transition matrix governs how fast the walk forgets its start, so mixing time is a reciprocal eigenvalue — the same structure as every relaxation time in this compendium. Expander graphs have large gaps and mix in 𝑂(log 𝑛) steps.	Mixing time is a reciprocal spectral gap.	Exact linear.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	52	SIS Epidemic on Networks	linearized	spectral,coupled,graph,control	A linear coupling term with a saturating nonlinearity.	Linearising about the disease-free state gives the Jacobian −𝐼+ 𝜆𝐴, whose leading eigenvalue crosses zero at 𝜆= 1/𝜆1 — entry 49’s threshold, derived directly. The nonlinearity only bounds the growth.	The threshold is a Jacobian eigenvalue crossing; the coupling is 𝐴.	Linearised.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	53	Percolation Threshold	exact_linear	dynamical,graph	A branching ratio condition in the first two moments.	Random node removal fails to destroy scale-free networks because ⟨𝑘2⟩diverges, sending 𝑝𝑐→0. It is the same moment condition as Molloy–Reed, applied to a thinned network.	Percolation is a moment-based branching condition.	Exact linear.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	54	Targeted Attack Vulnerability	exact_linear	variational,dynamical	The complementary case to entry 53.	Removing the highest-degree nodes cuts the second moment fastest, so the branching ratio drops immediately — robustness to random failure and fragility to targeted attack are two consequences of one heavy tail. That duality is the field’s most cited result.	Targeted removal attacks the second moment directly.	Exact linear.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	55	Cascading Failure Load Model	linearized	probabilistic,dynamical,coupled,graph	An iterative redistribution on the network.	Each redistribution is a linear map determined by the current topology, but the topology changes as nodes fail — so the process is a sequence of linear maps on a shrinking graph. The threshold behaviour comes from that coupling, not from any single map.	Cascades are a sequence of linear redistributions on a changing graph.	Linearised.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	56	Kuramoto Synchronisation on a Network	linearized	operator,dynamical,coupled,graph	Phase coupling through the adjacency matrix.	Linearising about the synchronised state gives ̇𝜃= −𝐾𝐿𝜃— exactly Laplacian diffusion, so synchro- nisation speed is governed by 𝜆2. The all-to-all case reduces to a rank-one mean-field.	Near synchrony the dynamics are Laplacian diffusion.	Linearised.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	57	Master Stability Function	exact_linear	spectral,dynamical,coupled,graph,exact_numeric	A ratio of the extreme Laplacian eigenvalues.	Stability of the synchronous state requires every nonzero Laplacian eigenvalue scaled by the coupling to lie in a stability interval, so a small eigenvalue ratio makes that possible for a wider coupling range. It separates the dynamics (the stability interval) from the topology (the spectrum) exactly.	Synchronisability is a Laplacian eigenratio; dynamics and topology separate.	Exact linear (linearised).
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	58	Structural Controllability	exact_linear	spectral,dynamical,graph,control,combinatorial	A combinatorial matching quantity, not a spectral one.	Liu, Slotine, and Barabási’s result is that the number of required driver nodes follows from a maxi- mum bipartite matching on the network — the unmatched nodes must be driven. It is a rank statement in a combinatorial rather than spectral sense, computable in polynomial time by Hopcroft–Karp.	Controllability is a maximum matching, not an eigenvalue computation.	Exact linear (combinatorial rank).
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	59	Network Eﬀiciency	exact_linear	operator,graph,geometric	A mean reciprocal distance.	Exactly the average of the harmonic centralities, so it handles disconnected graphs gracefully. Local eﬀiciency computed on neighbourhoods measures fault tolerance.	Eﬀiciency is a mean reciprocal of the distance matrix.	Exact linear.
77	Network Science — Calculus Edition	60	Temporal and Multilayer Networks	exact_linear	operator,coupled,graph,geometric	A fourth-order tensor rather than a matrix.	Layers and time steps add indices, so the natural object is a tensor and the supra-adjacency repre- sentation flattens it into a large block matrix. Tensor decompositions generalise the SVD, but non-commuting time-ordered products mean paths must respect temporal ordering — reachability is no longer a simple matrix power.	Multilayer networks are tensors; supra-adjacency flattens them to matrices.	Exact linear (tensor).
79	Information Theory — Calculus Edition	1	Shannon Entropy	exact_linear	probabilistic,informational	An inner product of a probability vector with a surprisal vector.	Exactly p⊤s with 𝑠𝑖= −log 𝑝𝑖— linear in p given s, and concave in p overall. Its maximum on the simplex is at the uniform distribution, which is a symmetry argument.	Entropy is an inner product with the surprisal vector.	Exact linear in surprisal.
79	Information Theory — Calculus Edition	2	Joint Entropy	exact_linear	operator,probabilistic,informational	The same functional applied to the joint probability matrix.	Treats the joint distribution as a flattened vector, so all the entropy identities carry over. Subadditivity 𝐻(𝑋, 𝑌) ≤𝐻(𝑋) + 𝐻(𝑌) is exactly the non-negativity of mutual information.	Joint entropy flattens the matrix and applies the same functional.	Exact linear in surprisal.
79	Information Theory — Calculus Edition	3	Conditional Entropy	exact_linear	informational,exact_numeric	A weighted average of row entropies.	Exactly ∑𝑖𝑝𝑖𝐻(𝑌∣𝑋= 𝑖) — an inner product of the marginal with the vector of row entropies. Conditioning never increases entropy, which is Jensen’s inequality applied to the concave entropy function.	Conditional entropy is a marginal-weighted average of row entropies.	Exact linear.
79	Information Theory — Calculus Edition	4	Chain Rule for Entropy	exact_linear	probabilistic,informational	An exact additive decomposition.	Follows immediately from the logarithm turning the product 𝑝𝑖𝑗= 𝑝𝑖𝑝𝑗∣𝑖into a sum — the whole chain rule is that one algebraic fact. It extends to any number of variables by iteration.	The chain rule is log-additivity of a probability factorisation.	Exact linear in logs.
79	Information Theory — Calculus Edition	5	Mutual Information	exact_linear	operator,informational	A divergence between the joint matrix and its rank-one margin product.	Zero exactly when 𝑃= pq⊤— that is, when the joint matrix has rank one. Mutual information is therefore a measure of departure from rank one, which is the same object as in every independence test in this compendium.	Mutual information measures departure from a rank-one joint.	Exact linear.
79	Information Theory — Calculus Edition	6	Mutual Information Identities	exact_linear	informational	Linear relations among four entropy quantities.	The Venn diagram of information is exact for two variables because the identities are linear — three quantities determine the fourth. For three or more variables the “interaction information” can be negative, so the diagram picture breaks.	The identities are linear relations on an entropy vector.	Exact linear.
79	Information Theory — Calculus Edition	7	Conditional Mutual Information	exact_linear	informational,exact_numeric	A linear contrast of two conditional entropies.	Non-negative always, but it can exceed the unconditional 𝐼(𝑋; 𝑌) — conditioning can create depen- dence, which is exactly the collider effect from causal inference. That non-monotonicity is why information diagrams mislead.	The quantity is a linear entropy contrast; conditioning can increase it.	Exact linear.
79	Information Theory — Calculus Edition	8	Kullback–Leibler Divergence	exact_linear	informational,geometric	An inner product of p with a log-ratio vector.	Linear in 𝑃given the log-ratio, but neither symmetric nor a metric. Its non-negativity is Jensen’s inequality, and it is the exponential family’s natural geometry.	KL is a linear functional of 𝑃against a log-ratio vector.	Exact linear in 𝑃.
79	Information Theory — Calculus Edition	9	Jensen–Shannon Divergence	exact_linear	variational,informational,geometric	A symmetrised divergence about a linear midpoint.	The midpoint 𝑀= (𝑃+ 𝑄)/2 is a convex combination, and the symmetrisation makes √ JSD a genuine metric. It equals the mutual information between the sample and its source label.	Symmetrisation about a linear midpoint yields a metric.	Exact linear.
79	Information Theory — Calculus Edition	10	Cross Entropy	exact_linear	probabilistic,informational,dynamical,learning,control	An inner product of the true distribution with the model’s surprisal.	Exactly linear in 𝑃against −log q, and it decomposes into an irreducible entropy plus a divergence. Minimising cross entropy is minimising KL, since 𝐻(𝑃) is fixed — which is why it is the standard classification loss.	Cross entropy is a linear functional splitting into entropy plus divergence.	Exact linear.
79	Information Theory — Calculus Edition	11	Differential Entropy	exact_linear	operator,informational	The continuous analogue, not coordinate invariant.	Under a linear change of variables y = 𝐴x it shifts by log |det 𝐴| — so differential entropy transforms by a log determinant, which is exactly why it can be negative and why only differences of it are meaningful. Mutual information is invariant, because the determinants cancel.	Differential entropy shifts by log |det 𝐴|; mutual information is invariant.	Exact linear.
79	Information Theory — Calculus Edition	12	Maximum Entropy Distributions	exact_linear	variational,probabilistic,informational	A concave maximisation with linear moment constraints.	The constraints are exactly linear functionals of the distribution, so the solution is an exponential family 𝑝∝exp(∑𝜆𝑘𝑓𝑘) with the Lagrange multipliers as natural parameters. Every named distribution in statistics arises this way from some set of linear constraints.	MaxEnt is concave maximisation over linear constraints; the solution is exponential.	Exact linear.
79	Information Theory — Calculus Edition	13	Gaussian Entropy	exact_linear	spectral,operator,probabilistic,informational	A log determinant of the covariance matrix.	Exactly 1 2 log det Σ up to a constant, so Gaussian entropy is a spectral quantity — the sum of log eigenvalues. Maximum entropy for a fixed covariance means the Gaussian is the least assumptive choice.	Gaussian entropy is a log determinant — a sum of log eigenvalues.	Exact linear.
79	Information Theory — Calculus Edition	14	Rényi Entropy	exact_linear	probabilistic,informational	A one-parameter family of ℓ𝛼-norm-based entropies.	Exactly 𝛼 1−𝛼log ‖p‖𝛼, so the family is indexed by which norm is used. 𝛼→1 gives Shannon, 𝛼= 2 gives collision entropy, 𝛼→∞gives min-entropy. The LKVexa Compendium — Calculus Edition 15	Rényi entropies are log norms of the probability vector.	Exact linear.
79	Information Theory — Calculus Edition	15	Min-Entropy	exact_linear	probabilistic,informational,graph	The ℓ∞limit of the family.	Determined by the single largest probability, so it is the most conservative entropy measure — which is why cryptography uses it for randomness extraction. It lower-bounds every other Rényi entropy.	Min-entropy is the log of the sup norm.	Exact linear.
79	Information Theory — Calculus Edition	16	Tsallis Entropy	structural_analogue	informational	A non-additive entropy family.	Non-additive for independent systems — 𝑆𝑞(𝐴, 𝐵) = 𝑆𝑞(𝐴) + 𝑆𝑞(𝐵) + (1 −𝑞)𝑆𝑞(𝐴)𝑆𝑞(𝐵) — so it deliberately breaks the additivity that makes Shannon entropy natural. That is its point and its controversy.	Tsallis entropy breaks additivity by construction.	Structural analogue.
79	Information Theory — Calculus Edition	17	Fisher Information	exact_linear	operator,probabilistic,informational,geometric	The expected outer product of the score — a matrix.	Exactly 𝐸[ss⊤] with s the score vector, so it is a positive semidefinite matrix whose inverse bounds estimator covariance (Cramér–Rao). It is the local curvature of the KL divergence — the Fisher–Rao metric.	Fisher information is a score covariance matrix and a local KL metric.	Exact linear.
79	Information Theory — Calculus Edition	18	Data Processing Inequality	exact_linear	operator,probabilistic,informational	A monotonicity under composition of stochastic matrices.	Post-processing is multiplication by a stochastic matrix, and mutual information cannot increase under it — a contraction property. It is why no clever processing can create information that was not in the data.	Processing is stochastic matrix multiplication; information contracts.	Exact linear.
79	Information Theory — Calculus Edition	19	Fano’s Inequality	exact_linear	variational,probabilistic,informational,exact_numeric	A lower bound on the error probability from conditional entropy.	Linear in 𝑃𝑒up to the binary entropy term, so it converts an information quantity into an error bound. It is the workhorse of every converse proof in the field, including the channel coding converse.	Fano bounds error linearly in terms of residual entropy.	Exact linear.
79	Information Theory — Calculus Edition	20	Source Coding Theorem	exact_linear	probabilistic,informational,dynamical	A lower bound on expected code length.	Expected length is an inner product of the probability vector with the length vector, so the theorem says that inner product cannot fall below the entropy. It is the converse half of compression.	Expected length is an inner product bounded below by entropy.	Exact linear.
79	Information Theory — Calculus Edition	21	Kraft–McMillan Inequality	exact_linear	variational,informational,dynamical	A feasibility constraint on the length vector.	Exactly the condition for a prefix code to exist, and it defines a convex feasible region in length space. Minimising the inner product p⊤ℓover that region gives ℓ𝑖= −log 𝑝𝑖— a constrained optimisation whose solution is the entropy.	Kraft defines a convex region; optimal coding minimises a linear objective on it.	Exact linear.
79	Information Theory — Calculus Edition	22	Shannon–Fano–Elias Code Length	exact_linear	variational,informational,dynamical,exact_numeric	A rounded-up optimal length.	The ceiling costs at most one bit per symbol, so the code is within two bits of the entropy. Integer constraints are the only reason optimal coding does not achieve the bound exactly.	Rounding the optimal real length costs a bounded excess.	Exact linear.
79	Information Theory — Calculus Edition	23	Huffman Coding	exact_linear	variational,informational	A greedy tree construction that is provably optimal.	Optimality follows from an exchange argument — the two least probable symbols must be siblings at maximum depth. The resulting expected length is within one bit of entropy, and the greedy step is justified by a matroid-like exchange property.	Huffman is a greedy construction justified by an exchange argument.	Exact linear.
79	Information Theory — Calculus Edition	24	Arithmetic Coding	exact_linear	probabilistic,informational,dynamical,control,exact_numeric	A nested sequence of interval subdivisions.	Each symbol maps the current interval to a subinterval by an aﬀine map with scale 𝑝𝑖— so the final interval’s length is exactly the sequence probability, and its log is the code length. Arithmetic coding is a composition of aﬀine contractions.	Coding composes aﬀine interval contractions; length is the log of the product.	Exact linear.
79	Information Theory — Calculus Edition	25	Asymptotic Equipartition Property	exact_linear	probabilistic,informational	A law of large numbers on the log probability.	Exactly the sample mean of the surprisal converging to its expectation, so the AEP is the LLN applied to a linear functional. It is the reason typical sets exist.	The AEP is the law of large numbers on surprisal.	Exact linear.
79	Information Theory — Calculus Edition	26	Typical Set Size	exact_linear	probabilistic,informational,combinatorial	An exponentially small subset carrying almost all probability.	Out of |𝒳| 𝑛sequences only 2𝑛𝐻matter, so compression works by indexing that subset — the log of the count is 𝑛times a linear functional. Everything in source coding follows from this counting.	The log of the typical set size is 𝑛times a linear functional.	Exact linear in logs.
79	Information Theory — Calculus Edition	27	Lempel–Ziv Universal Coding	structural_analogue	variational,informational,combinatorial	A dictionary construction achieving the entropy rate without knowing it.	Universality means the same algorithm is asymptotically optimal for every stationary ergodic source — a remarkable property, and it works because the parsing count grows as 𝑛/ log 𝑛for a source of entropy rate 𝐻. The construction itself is combinatorial.	The parsing is combinatorial; the limit is the entropy rate.	Structural analogue.
79	Information Theory — Calculus Edition	28	Entropy Rate	exact_linear	informational	A per-symbol limit of the joint entropy.	Equivalently lim 𝐻(𝑋𝑛∣𝑋1, … , 𝑋𝑛−1) — the two definitions agree for stationary processes, which is a Cesàro convergence statement. It is the compression limit for a source with memory.	The entropy rate is a Cesàro limit of a linear functional.	Exact linear.
79	Information Theory — Calculus Edition	29	Markov Source Entropy Rate	exact_linear	spectral,probabilistic,informational	A stationary-distribution-weighted average of row entropies.	Exactly 𝜋⊤h where 𝜋is the Perron eigenvector of 𝑃and h the vector of row entropies — an inner product of an eigenvector with an entropy vector. The whole calculation is spectral plus a contraction.	The rate contracts a Perron eigenvector with row entropies.	Exact linear.
79	Information Theory — Calculus Edition	30	Minimum Description Length	exact_linear	dynamical,combinatorial	A two-part code length minimisation.	Exactly a sum of two code lengths, so model selection is a linear trade-off in bits — and the model complexity term is asymptotically 𝑘 2 log 𝑛, matching BIC. It is Occam’s razor as a linear objective. The LKVexa Compendium — Calculus Edition 30	MDL minimises a sum of two lengths.	Exact linear.
79	Information Theory — Calculus Edition	31	Channel Capacity	exact_linear	operator,probabilistic,informational	A concave maximisation over the input simplex.	Mutual information is concave in the input distribution for a fixed channel matrix, so the maximum is unique and computable — the Blahut–Arimoto algorithm alternates two closed-form updates. The channel itself is a stochastic matrix.	Capacity is concave maximisation over a simplex; the channel is a matrix. The LKVexa Compendium — Calculus Edition 31	Exact linear.
79	Information Theory — Calculus Edition	32	Binary Symmetric Channel	exact_linear	operator,variational,informational,geometric	A channel matrix ( 1−𝑝 𝑝 𝑝 1−𝑝).	Symmetric, so the capacity-achieving input is uniform by a symmetry argument and the capacity is one minus the noise entropy. At 𝑝= 1/2 the matrix has rank one and the capacity is zero — a rank statement.	Symmetry gives a uniform optimal input; 𝑝= 1/2 makes the matrix rank one.	Exact linear.
79	Information Theory — Calculus Edition	33	Binary Entropy Function	exact_linear	informational,geometric	A concave function on [0, 1].	Maximised at 𝑝= 1/2 and symmetric about it, and its concavity is what makes every capacity formula involving it well behaved. It appears throughout coding theory as the exponent in volume bounds.	The function is the two-point entropy; concavity drives the optimisations.	Exact linear in surprisal.
79	Information Theory — Calculus Edition	34	Binary Erasure Channel	exact_linear	informational	A channel with a third output symbol.	Capacity is exactly the fraction of unerased symbols, because erasures are known — the receiver’s uncertainty is entirely about which positions were lost, not about their values. That makes the capacity linear in 𝜀, unlike the BSC.	Erasure capacity is exactly linear in the erasure rate.	Exact linear.
79	Information Theory — Calculus Edition	35	Shannon–Hartley Capacity	exact_linear	probabilistic,informational	The capacity of a bandlimited Gaussian channel.	Linear in bandwidth and logarithmic in signal-to-noise ratio, so bandwidth is the cheaper resource. It follows from entry 36 with 𝐵independent uses per second.	Capacity is linear in bandwidth, log in SNR.	Exact linear.
